Solution Explanation  
We have the three different letters a , b , c, each of them appears exactly once.

Let target = "abc".  
Compare the current row with target:

• If the two strings are identical, we are already done – answer YES.  
• Otherwise some positions are wrong.

Because every letter is different, the number of wrong positions can only be  
0, 2, or 3. ( 1 is impossible: if one place is wrong, some other place must contain
a duplicated letter.)

• 2 wrong positions – swap those two cards and the row becomes abc → YES.  
• 3 wrong positions – you would have to move every card, one swap is not enough → NO.

So the answer is YES iff the number of positions in which s[i] ≠ "abc"[i] is at most 2.

Complexity  
Length of the string is fixed (3).  
Time : O(1) per test case.  
Memory: O(1).

Reference implementation (C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;                     // number of test cases
    if (!(cin >> t)) return 0;
    while (t--) {
        string s;              // the given permutation
        cin >> s;

        int wrong = 0;         // positions that differ from "abc"
        const string target = "abc";
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
            if (s[i] != target[i]) ++wrong;

        cout << (wrong <= 2 ? "YES\n" : "NO\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In Android a Toolbar is owned by the hosting Activity, but the view that will become that Toolbar can just as well be defined inside the Fragment’s layout.  
In practice the steps are:

1. Put a Toolbar widget in the fragment’s XML.  
2. Once the fragment’s view is created hand that Toolbar over to the Activity with setSupportActionBar().  
3. Tell the fragment that it will supply an options-menu (optional).  
4. When the fragment’s view is destroyed, give the Activity its original action bar back (good housekeeping).

Complete example
===============

build.gradle (app module)
```
dependencies {
    implementation 'androidx.appcompat:appcompat:1.6.1'   // or latest
}
```

res/layout/fragment_example.xml
```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent">

    <!-- Toolbar that will become the ActionBar -->
    <com.google.android.material.appbar.Toolbar
        android:id="@+id/toolbar"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
        android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
        app:titleTextColor="@android:color/white"
        app:popupTheme="@style/ThemeOverlay.AppCompat.Light"/>

    <!-- the rest of the fragment UI -->

</androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout>
```

Fragment class
```java
public class ExampleFragment extends Fragment {

    private Toolbar toolbar;

    @Override
    public View onCreateView(LayoutInflater inflater,
                             ViewGroup container,
                             Bundle savedInstanceState) {

        View root = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_example, container, false);

        toolbar = root.findViewById(R.id.toolbar);

        // Hand the toolbar to the activity
        AppCompatActivity act = (AppCompatActivity) requireActivity();
        act.setSupportActionBar(toolbar);

        // Optional: enable back arrow
        ActionBar ab = act.getSupportActionBar();
        if (ab != null) {
            ab.setDisplayHomeAsUpEnabled(true);
            ab.setDisplayShowHomeEnabled(true);
        }

        setHasOptionsMenu(true);   // Fragment will provide its own menu
        return root;
    }

    @Override
    public void onCreateOptionsMenu(Menu menu, MenuInflater inflater) {
        inflater.inflate(R.menu.menu_fragment, menu);
        super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean onOptionsItemSelected(@NonNull MenuItem item) {
        if (item.getItemId() == android.R.id.home) {
            requireActivity().onBackPressed();      // up-navigation
            return true;
        }
        // handle other items …
        return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item);
    }

    @Override
    public void onDestroyView() {
        // Give the activity back its previous toolbar (good practice)
        AppCompatActivity act = (AppCompatActivity) requireActivity();
        act.setSupportActionBar(null);
        super.onDestroyView();
    }
}
```

What if you don’t need it to replace the ActionBar?
--------------------------------------------------
If you simply want a toolbar-looking view inside the fragment (for instance inside a scrolling screen) you can skip setSupportActionBar():

```java
Toolbar tb = root.findViewById(R.id.toolbar);
tb.setTitle("Local title");
tb.inflateMenu(R.menu.menu_fragment);
tb.setOnMenuItemClickListener(item -> {
     // handle clicks
     return true;
});
```

Key points to remember
----------------------
• The host Activity must extend AppCompatActivity (or ComponentActivity + Toolbar APIs in AndroidX).  
• Call setHasOptionsMenu(true) before inflating/using the fragment’s menu.  
• Clean up in onDestroyView(), otherwise the Activity keeps a reference to a dead view after the fragment is removed.

```js
/**
 * Iteratively looks for the first object whose own property `name`
 * equals `targetName`.  No recursion is used – only an explicit stack.
 *
 * @param {Object} root        – the object to search in
 * @param {string} targetName  – the value to look for in any `.name` prop
 * @returns {{object: Object, path: string[]} | null}
 *          If found:  { object: <the object>, path: ['key1','key2', ...] }
 *          If absent: null
 */
function findByName(root, targetName) {
  if (root == null || typeof root !== 'object') return null;

  // Each entry: { node: <current object>, path: <array of keys leading here> }
  const stack   = [{ node: root, path: [] }];
  const visited = new Set([root]);        // Guards against circular refs

  while (stack.length) {
    const { node, path } = stack.pop();

    // 1. Is this the object we want?
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(node, 'name') &&
        node.name === targetName) {
      return { object: node, path };
    }

    // 2. Add children (own enumerable properties) to the stack
    for (const key of Object.keys(node)) {
      const child = node[key];
      if (child && typeof child === 'object' && !visited.has(child)) {
        visited.add(child);
        stack.push({ node: child, path: path.concat(key) });
      }
    }
  }

  return null; // not found
}
```

Example use:

```js
const data = {
  name: 'root',
  users: [
    { name: 'alice' },
    { name: 'bob', extra: { name: 'target' } }
  ],
  config: {
    theme: { colors: { primary: { name: 'blueish' } } }
  }
};

const result = findByName(data, 'target');
if (result) {
  console.log('Found object:', result.object);            // { name: 'target' }
  console.log('Path as array:', result.path);             // ['users', 1, 'extra']
  console.log('Path as dot-string:', result.path.join('.')); // users.1.extra
} else {
  console.log('Not found');
}
```

How it works (in a nutshell)
• A stack is used to emulate the usual depth-first recursion.
• Each stack entry carries the object to inspect and the key-path that reached it.  
• As soon as an object with `name === targetName` is met, the current path is returned.  
• A `Set` of visited objects prevents infinite loops caused by cycles.

