Solution idea  
Let  

c i = 1   if S i = ‘1’   (toy goes to Alice)  
c i = –1  if S i = ‘0’   (toy goes to Bob)  

For a price array v 1 ≤ v 2 ≤ … ≤ v N (v i ≥ 0) the difference of the two totals is  

D = Σ c i v i .  

The distribution is called fair when |D| ≤ v N for every possible non-decreasing non-negative
array v.

Write the two required inequalities

1)  Σ c i v i – v N ≤ 0  
2) –Σ c i v i – v N ≤ 0 .

For (1) put  

d i = c i   (i < N),   d N = c N – 1 ,    so that the left side is Σ d i v i .  
For every suffix k … N let  

D k = Σ i = k … N d i .  

If some D k were positive we could choose v k = v k+1 = … = v N = M (very large) and
Σ d i v i would go to +∞, violating (1).
Thus we need D k ≤ 0 for every suffix.  
Conversely, if all D k ≤ 0 then with u 1 = v 1 ,
u j = v j – v j-1 (u j ≥ 0) we have  

Σ d i v i = Σ j u j (Σ i = j … N d i ) = Σ j u j D j ≤ 0 ,

so the inequality is satisfied.  
Therefore (1) is equivalent to

      Σ i = k … N d i ≤ 0   for every k .          (A)

Doing the same for (2) gives

      Σ i = k … N (–c i ) – 1 ≤ 0    ⇒   –Σ i = k … N c i ≤ 1 .   (B)

Because d i differs from c i only in the last position,

Σ i = k … N d i = Σ i = k … N c i – 1 ,

so (A) becomes Σ c suffix ≤ 1 ,
while (B) becomes Σ c suffix ≥ –1.

Hence the distribution is fair iff

For every suffix k … N           –1 ≤  Σ i = k … N c i  ≤ 1.

But Σ c suffix is simply
(# of ‘1’ in the suffix) – (# of ‘0’ in the suffix).

So:  the difference between the counts of ‘1’ and ‘0’ in every
suffix must be at most 1 in absolute value.

Algorithm  
• traverse the string from right to left, maintaining  
  diff = (#1 – #0) seen so far (i.e. on the current suffix).  
• after updating diff, if diff < –1 or diff > 1 → answer is NO.  
• if all positions are processed without breaking → YES.

Complexities  
O(N) time and O(1) additional memory per test case;  
Σ N over all cases ≤ 3·10⁵ so it easily fits in time limits.

Reference implementation in C
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void)
{
    int T;
    scanf("%d", &T);
    while (T--)
    {
        int N;
        scanf("%d", &N);
        char S[N + 2];
        scanf("%s", S);

        int diff = 0;      /* (#1 – #0) on current suffix */
        int ok   = 1;

        for (int i = N - 1; i >= 0; --i)
        {
            diff += (S[i] == '1') ? 1 : -1;
            if (diff < -1 || diff > 1)
            {
                ok = 0;
                break;
            }
        }
        printf(ok ? "YES\n" : "NO\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```
The program follows exactly the condition derived above and prints YES if the distribution is fair, NO otherwise.

Solution Explanation  
For every pair there can be  

• 0 rated players (UU)  
• 1 rated + 1 unrated player (RU) – this is what we like  
• 2 rated players (RR) – both rated players are counted in the required answer  

Let  
R = X                 – number of rated players  
U = 2·N – X           – number of unrated players  

Pair as many rated players as possible with unrated players.  
The number of such “good” pairs is min(R , U).

Case-1 : R ≤ U  
Every rated player gets an unrated opponent ⇒ no RR pair ⇒ answer = 0.

Case-2 : R > U  
All U unrated players are already paired with U rated players.  
Still left = R – U = 2·X – 2·N rated players.  
They must be paired among themselves:  
RR pairs = (R – U)/2 = X – N  
Rated players inside those pairs = 2·(X – N).

So the answer for one test case is  

ans = max(0 , 2·X – 2·N)      (or 2·max(0 , X – N)).



Complexities  
Time  : O(T) (constant work per test case)  
Memory: O(1)



Reference implementation (C)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int T;
    if (scanf("%d", &T) != 1) return 0;

    while (T--)
    {
        int N, X;
        scanf("%d %d", &N, &X);

        int answer = 0;
        if (X > N)                /* more rated than pairs available for them */
            answer = 2 * (X - N); /* 2 rated players in every extra RR pair */

        printf("%d\n", answer);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Create an auxiliary (binary) set that stores the one-to-one link between the two second–dimension sets and then use that set in a conditional assignment.

Sets
    A               first dimension
    B               second dimension of P1
    C               second dimension of P2
    map(B,C)        ‘1’ iff element B is mapped to element C  ;

*---parameter you already have
Parameter P1(A,B)   values over A × B ;

*---parameter you want to create
Parameter P2(A,C)   values over A × C ;

*---copy the data
P2(A,C) = sum( B$map(B,C) , P1(A,B) );

Explanation

•  map(B,C) is 1 for exactly one pair (B,C), so for a given C the term  
   sum(B$map(B,C), …) contains only that single B and simply copies its value.  

•  For C’s for which no mapping exists the conditional $map(B,C) is always 0,  
   so nothing is added and P2 is left at its default value (0).

If you prefer P2 to be defined only where a mapping exists you can write

P2(A,C)$(sum(map(B,C),1)) = sum(B$map(B,C), P1(A,B));

or, equivalently,

loop( map(B,C),
     P2(A,C) = P1(A,B);
);

Either formulation produces parameter P2(A,C) with exactly the values of P1(A,B) determined by the B↔C mapping.

