Below is a minimal, production-ready “mini-project” composed of three files.  
It

• connects to a MySQL database called Bob  
• receives HTTP-POST parameters field1 and field2  
• stores them safely with a prepared statement

———— 1.  SQL you run only once —————
```sql
-- Run this from the MySQL client or phpMyAdmin
CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS Bob CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;
USE Bob;

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS posts (
    id        INT UNSIGNED AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    field1    VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    field2    VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    createdAt TIMESTAMP    NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
```

———— 2.  project/config.php —————
```php
<?php
/* Centralised credentials – NEVER commit this to public version control */
define('DB_HOST', '127.0.0.1');   // or 'localhost'
define('DB_USER', 'YOUR_MYSQL_USER');
define('DB_PASS', 'YOUR_MYSQL_PASSWORD');
define('DB_NAME', 'Bob');

/* Open the connection with PDO */
try {
    $pdo = new PDO(
        'mysql:host='.DB_HOST.';dbname='.DB_NAME.';charset=utf8mb4',
        DB_USER,
        DB_PASS,
        [ PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION ]
    );
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    http_response_code(500);
    exit('Database connection failed.');
}
```

———— 3.  project/insert.php —————
```php
<?php
// The endpoint that receives and stores the data
require __DIR__.'/config.php';

/* Accept only POST */
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] !== 'POST') {
    http_response_code(405);           // Method Not Allowed
    header('Allow: POST');
    exit('Use POST');
}

/* Basic validation */
$field1 = trim($_POST['field1'] ?? '');
$field2 = trim($_POST['field2'] ?? '');

if ($field1 === '' || $field2 === '') {
    http_response_code(400);           // Bad Request
    exit('Both field1 and field2 are required.');
}

/* Insert with a prepared statement */
$sql = 'INSERT INTO posts (field1, field2) VALUES (:f1, :f2)';
$stmt = $pdo->prepare($sql);
$stmt->execute([
    ':f1' => $field1,
    ':f2' => $field2
]);

/* Return a tiny JSON response */
header('Content-Type: application/json');
echo json_encode([
    'success' => true,
    'id'      => $pdo->lastInsertId()
]);
```

———— 4.  (optional) quick-test form —————
Save as test.html next to insert.php:
```html
<form action="insert.php" method="post">
    Field 1: <input name="field1"><br>
    Field 2: <input name="field2"><br>
    <button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>
```

Deployment notes
• Put the project folder inside your web-root (e.g. /var/www/html).  
• Make sure PHP >= 7.4 and the PDO-MySQL extension are enabled.  
• Keep config.php outside the public repo or load credentials via environment variables.

Below is a minimal, self-contained Chrome extension (Manifest V3) that adds a toolbar button.  
When the button is pressed it grabs the full HTML of the currently-active tab and downloads it as a normal “.html” file.

Directory layout (all files go in the same folder, e.g. “save-page-extension/”)

• manifest.json  
• background.js

──────────────── manifest.json ────────────────
```json
{
  "name":        "Save This Page",
  "description": "One-click download of the current tab’s HTML.",
  "version":     "1.0",
  "manifest_version": 3,

  "permissions": [
    "activeTab",          // grants temporary access to tab content
    "scripting",          // lets us inject code
    "downloads"           // lets us create a download
  ],

  "action": {
    "default_title": "Save page as HTML",
    "default_icon":  {
      "16": "icon16.png",
      "32": "icon32.png",
      "48": "icon48.png",
      "128": "icon128.png"
    }
  },

  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  }
}
```

(If you don’t care about an icon, you can omit the `default_icon` block.)

──────────────── background.js ────────────────
```javascript
// Runs in the background (service worker).

chrome.action.onClicked.addListener(async (tab) => {
  if (!tab?.id) return;

  try {
    /* 1. Inject a small function into the page that returns
          the full HTML (outerHTML of <html>).               */
    const [{ result: pageHTML }] = await chrome.scripting.executeScript({
      target: { tabId: tab.id },
      world:  "MAIN",
      func:   () => document.documentElement.outerHTML
    });

    /* 2. Turn the string into a Blob & an object URL. */
    const blob = new Blob([pageHTML], { type: "text/html" });
    const url  = URL.createObjectURL(blob);

    /* 3. Suggest a file name based on the page’s host. */
    const fallbackName = "saved-page.html";
    let filename;
    try {
      filename = new URL(tab.url)
        .hostname
        .replace(/[^\w.-]/g, "_")         // sanitise
        .replace(/^www_/, "") + ".html";
    } catch {
      filename = fallbackName;
    }

    /* 4. Download it. `saveAs:true` pops a normal “Save as…” dialog. */
    chrome.downloads.download(
      {
        url,
        filename,
        saveAs: true
      },
      () => {
        /* 5. Clean up the object URL a little later (after
              Chrome has finished reading it). */
        setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(url), 10000);
      }
    );
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Save-page extension error:", err);
  }
});
```

What it does (step-by-step)
1. When you click the extension’s toolbar icon, Chrome calls `chrome.action.onClicked`.  
2. The service worker injects a very small script into the current tab that simply returns `document.documentElement.outerHTML`.  
3. The returned string is packed into a Blob.  
4. The Blob is turned into a temporary object-URL.  
5. The `chrome.downloads.download()` API starts a download using that URL and suggests a filename.  
6. Ten seconds later the object-URL is revoked to free memory.

How to install & test
1. Put the two files (plus optional icons) in a folder.  
2. In Chrome, open `chrome://extensions`.  
3. Enable “Developer mode”, click “Load unpacked”, and select the folder.  
4. Visit any page, click the new toolbar button, choose a location in the “Save As…” dialog, and the page HTML will be saved to disk.

Permissions explained
activeTab  – gives the extension temporary access to the exact tab you clicked in.  
scripting   – allows us to execute `document.documentElement.outerHTML` inside that tab.  
downloads   – needed to create the download.

That’s the entire, working plugin.

