ru->common.rxdataF[i] = (int32_t*)malloc16_clear(sizeof(int32_t)*(NUMBER_RX_BUFFERS*fp->symbols_per_slot*fp->ofdm_symbol_size) );

output:
Below is a compact, self-contained piece of C code that can be dropped into the OpenAirInterface (or any other) project to examine the content of ru->common.rxdataF.  
The function iterates over:

• Antenna index (0 … ru->nb_rx-1)  
• Buffer index              (0 … NUMBER_RX_BUFFERS-1)  
• OFDM symbol index         (0 … fp->symbols_per_slot-1)  
• Sub-carrier index         (0 … fp->ofdm_symbol_size-1)

and prints the complex value contained in every 32-bit word (low 16 bits = I, high 16 bits = Q).

```c
/********************************************************************
*  file : dump_rxdataF.c / .h
********************************************************************/
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

/*--- Small helpers to extract I/Q from one 32-bit word ------------*/
static inline int16_t RX_RE(int32_t w) { return (int16_t)(w & 0x0000FFFF); }
static inline int16_t RX_IM(int32_t w) { return (int16_t)(w >> 16);       }

/*------------------------------------------------------------------*
 *  dump_rxdataF()
 *------------------------------------------------------------------*
 *  rxdataF           : ru->common.rxdataF      (int32_t **)
 *  nb_rx             : ru->nb_rx               (antennas)
 *  number_rx_buffers : NUMBER_RX_BUFFERS
 *  symbols_per_slot  : fp->symbols_per_slot
 *  ofdm_symbol_size  : fp->ofdm_symbol_size
 *------------------------------------------------------------------*/
void dump_rxdataF(int32_t   **rxdataF,
                  unsigned    nb_rx,
                  unsigned    number_rx_buffers,
                  unsigned    symbols_per_slot,
                  unsigned    ofdm_symbol_size)
{
  const unsigned samps_per_buffer = symbols_per_slot * ofdm_symbol_size;

  for (unsigned ant = 0; ant < nb_rx; ++ant) {
    printf("\n================ Antenna %u ================\n", ant);

    for (unsigned buf = 0; buf < number_rx_buffers; ++buf) {
      printf("---- BUFFER %u ----\n", buf);

      int32_t *bptr = rxdataF[ant] + buf * samps_per_buffer;

      for (unsigned sym = 0; sym < symbols_per_slot; ++sym) {
        printf("sym %02u :", sym);

        for (unsigned k = 0; k < ofdm_symbol_size; ++k) {
          const int32_t  w  = bptr[sym * ofdm_symbol_size + k];
          const int16_t  i  = RX_RE(w);
          const int16_t  q  = RX_IM(w);
          printf(" (%6d,%6d)", i, q);
        }
        printf("\n");
      }
    }
  }
  fflush(stdout);
}
```

How to call it inside the RU driver (for example, right after `rx_fep()` so you look at frequency-domain samples):

```c
/*  Assume we are inside a fuction that already knows ru and fp  */
dump_rxdataF(ru->common.rxdataF,
             ru->nb_rx,
             NUMBER_RX_BUFFERS,
             fp->symbols_per_slot,
             fp->ofdm_symbol_size);
```

Notes / options
––––––––––––––––
1. Redirect to a file  
   If the console output is too big, simply do  
   `./lte-softmodem … > rxdataF_dump.txt`.

2. Print only a subset  
   Replace the innermost loops by ranges you are interested in (e.g., one
   OFDM symbol, or PRB of interest).

3. CSV logging  
   Switch the `printf` line to  
   `fprintf(fd,"%u,%u,%u,%u,%d,%d\n", ant, buf, sym, k, i, q);`  
   for easy import in Matlab/NumPy.

Compile/Test
––––––––––––
The file does not depend on anything except `<stdio.h>`/`<stdint.h>`, so it
compiles cleanly with

    gcc -Wall -O2 -c dump_rxdataF.c

or is simply included in any OpenAirInterface .c file and re-compiled.

