output:
Below is a direct, **logically equivalent** rewrite that tries to express the intent of the original routine with clearer names, standard library helpers and a more conventional control-flow.

```c
/*  Changes the global variable IsWindowedMode
 *      "/w" → windowed  (IsWindowedMode = 1)
 *      "/f" → fullscreen (IsWindowedMode = 0)
 *
 *  The scan is Shift-JIS safe: if a ‘/’ appears inside a
 *  2–byte SJIS character, it is ignored.
 */
void handleCmdLineArgs(char *cmdLine)
{
    size_t len        = strlen(cmdLine);   /* number of bytes in the string   */
    unsigned sjisStat = 0;                 /* state used by RK_CheckSJIS()    */

    /* we have to look one byte ahead, so stop at len-1                        */
    for (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < len; ++i)
    {
        /* ---- 1)  Handle SJIS multibyte characters ------------------------ */
        if (RK_CheckSJIS((sjisStat & 0xFFFFFF00) | (unsigned char)cmdLine[i]) == 1)
        {
            /* current byte is the lead byte of a 2-byte SJIS code point;
               skip the following trail byte so that it cannot be mistaken
               for a command-line switch character such as ‘/’.              */
            ++i;                 /* skip the trail byte */
            continue;
        }

        /* ---- 2)  Look for command-line switches -------------------------- */
        if (cmdLine[i] == '/')
        {
            /* case-insensitive comparison of the character that follows '/' */
            char option = (char)tolower((unsigned char)cmdLine[i + 1]);

            if (option == 'w')
                IsWindowedMode = 1;     /* windowed mode  */
            else if (option == 'f')
                IsWindowedMode = 0;     /* full-screen    */
        }
    }
}
```

What changed / why it is easier to read:

1. Replaced the hand-rolled `strlen` calculation with the standard `strlen`.
2. Swapped the unsigned-arithmetic gymnastics for ordinary `for` indices.
3. Used meaningful names (`cmdLine`, `len`, `sjisStat`, `option`) instead of generic `uVar2`, `uVar3`, etc.
4. Added comments that explain the purpose of `RK_CheckSJIS` and the reason we occasionally skip a byte.
5. Grouped the two logical parts of the loop: SJIS handling and option parsing.

Flexbox is perfect when you simply want an image and a block of text to sit next to one-another, but it cannot do the classic “newspaper wrap” (where the text starts in the narrow space to the right of the image and then widens after the bottom of the picture).  
That kind of contour-wrapping is only possible with float + (optionally) shape-outside, not with flexbox (or grid).

Below are two approaches:

────────────────────────────────────────
1. Flexbox ‑- side-by-side but NO wrap-under
────────────────────────────────────────
HTML
<div class="media">
    <img src="photo.jpg" alt="">
    <p>
        Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipisicing elit … (long text)
    </p>
</div>

CSS
.media{
    display:flex;           /* put the two items on the same row   */
    align-items:flex-start; /* top-align the image and paragraph    */
}
.media img{
    width:150px;            /* or whatever size you need           */
    flex:0 0 auto;          /* don’t grow/shrink                   */
    margin-right:1rem;      /* gap between image and text          */
}
.media p{ margin:0; }        /* kill default <p> top margin         */

/* Optional mobile “stacking” */
@media (max-width:600px){
    .media{
        flex-direction:column;  /* image on top, text below         */
    }
    .media img{
        margin:0 0 1rem;        /* gap when stacked                 */
    }
}

What you get:  

[IMAGE]  Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur…  
         (all lines stay the same width while the image is present;
          once the image ends, the lines still keep that width)

────────────────────────────────────────
2. Float (with or without shape-outside) ‑- true wrap-under
────────────────────────────────────────
HTML
<img class="float" src="photo.jpg" alt="">
<p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur … (long text)</p>

CSS
.float{
    float:left;             /* pull image out of normal flow       */
    width:150px;
    margin:0 1rem 1rem 0;   /* margin-right + margin-bottom        */
    /* Optional fancy contour */
    /* shape-outside: circle(50%);   or  inset(0 0 0 0 round 8px); */
}

Now the first few lines of the paragraph are narrow, sitting to the right of the picture, and once the image’s bottom is reached the remaining lines expand to the full width—exactly the “text wraps around the top and bottom of the picture” behaviour you asked for.

────────────────────────────────────────
Summary
────────────────────────────────────────
• Use flexbox if you only need the image and paragraph on the same row and you’re fine with the text staying in a fixed column beside the image.  
• Use float (possibly with shape-outside) if you need true text-wrap that enlarges after the picture ends. Flexbox (and CSS Grid) cannot provide that wrap-under effect by design.

Below is a self-contained JavaScript module that lets you approximate π in three different ways.  
You can copy the whole file, drop it into any browser console / Node.js script, and call the algorithm you prefer.

```js
/********************************************************************/
/*  π  CALCULATOR – three algorithms                                */
/*                                                                  */
/*  1.  Leibniz (slow, demonstrative)                               */
/*  2.  Nilakantha (medium speed, still simple)                     */
/*  3.  Gauss–Legendre (very fast, quadratic convergence)           */
/*                                                                  */
/********************************************************************/

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   1. Leibniz series  (π/4 = 1 – 1/3 + 1/5 – 1/7 + …)
 ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
function piLeibniz(iterations = 1_000_000) {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let k = 0; k < iterations; k++) {
    const term = ((k & 1) === 0 ? 1 : -1) / (2 * k + 1); // (−1)^k / (2k+1)
    sum += term;
  }
  return 4 * sum;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   2. Nilakantha series
      π = 3 + 4/(2·3·4) − 4/(4·5·6) + 4/(6·7·8) − …
 ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
function piNilakantha(iterations = 1_000_000) {
  let pi = 3;
  let sign = 1;                         // toggles + / −
  for (let k = 2, i = 0; i < iterations; k += 2, ++i) {
    const term = 4 / (k * (k + 1) * (k + 2));
    pi += sign * term;
    sign *= -1;
  }
  return pi;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   3. Gauss–Legendre algorithm (quadratic convergence)
 ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
function piGaussLegendre(iterations = 6) {
  let a = 1;
  let b = 1 / Math.sqrt(2);
  let t = 0.25;
  let p = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
    const nextA = (a + b) / 2;
    const nextB = Math.sqrt(a * b);
    const diff = a - nextA;
    const nextT = t - p * diff * diff;

    a = nextA;
    b = nextB;
    t = nextT;
    p *= 2;
  }

  const pi = ((a + b) ** 2) / (4 * t);
  return pi;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   Helper: pick an algorithm and display results
 ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
function demo() {
  const digits = (n) => n.toPrecision(15);

  console.log('Leibniz (1e6 iterations)    :', digits(piLeibniz(1_000_000)));
  console.log('Nilakantha (1e6 iterations) :', digits(piNilakantha(1_000_000)));
  console.log('Gauss–Legendre (6 iters)    :', digits(piGaussLegendre(6)));
}

/* If running directly (`node pi.js`) run the demo. */
if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && require.main === module) {
  demo();
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   Exports for modular usage (Node / ESM)
 ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
if (typeof module !== 'undefined') {
  module.exports = {
    piLeibniz,
    piNilakantha,
    piGaussLegendre
  };
}
```

How to use
==========

Browser console  
```js
// copy code above, then …
console.log(piGaussLegendre(7));  // → 3.141592653589794
```

Node.js  
```bash
node pi.js
```
or from another script:
```js
const { piGaussLegendre } = require('./pi');
console.log(piGaussLegendre(8));
```

Notes
-----

• Leibniz converges painfully slowly—great for teaching series but not for precision.  
• Nilakantha converges ~100× faster than Leibniz.  
• Gauss–Legendre doubles the number of correct digits each iteration; 6–8 iterations already hit double-precision limits.

Below is a compact, self-contained C# console program that

1. Rounds π to five fractional digits.
2. Converts that value to a fixed-point integer (so bitwise operations make sense).
3. XORs the number with a key once ( “encrypt” ) and then XORs it again with the same key ( “decrypt” ), proving that two identical XOR passes return the original value.

```csharp
using System;

namespace PiXorDemo
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            /* -----------------------------------------------------------
             * 1)  π to five fractional digits
             * -----------------------------------------------------------
             * Math.Round uses banker's rounding by default; MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero
             * ensures the classic 3.14159 result.
             */
            double pi = Math.Round(Math.PI, 5, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero);
            Console.WriteLine($"π (5 decimals) : {pi}");

            /* -----------------------------------------------------------
             * 2)  Convert to an integer so we can apply bitwise XOR
             *     (shift the decimal point five places → multiply by 100 000)
             * -----------------------------------------------------------
             */
            long fixedPointPi = (long)(pi * 100_000);   // 3.14159 → 314 159
            Console.WriteLine($"Fixed-point int : {fixedPointPi}");

            /* -----------------------------------------------------------
             * 3)  XOR twice with the same key
             * -----------------------------------------------------------
             */
            const long XorKey = 0x5A5A5A5AL;            // any non-zero bit pattern
            long once  = fixedPointPi ^ XorKey;         // first pass  (encrypt)
            long twice = once        ^ XorKey;          // second pass (decrypt)

            Console.WriteLine($"After 1st XOR  : {once}");
            Console.WriteLine($"After 2nd XOR  : {twice} (original value restored)");
        }
    }
}
```

Sample output

```
π (5 decimals) : 3.14159
Fixed-point int : 314159
After 1st XOR  : 1515559347
After 2nd XOR  : 314159 (original value restored)
```

Because `a ^ k ^ k == a` for any integers `a` and `k`, the second XOR pass brings you right back to the initial number.

Below is a complete, minimal, “zero-framework” example that shows how to turn the model facebook/blenderbot-400m-distill into a tiny web-chat that runs 100 % in the browser with transformers.js.  
No React, no Node, no build step – just one HTML file (plus an optional CSS file if you want to prettify it).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
1.  What you need
--------------------------------------------------------------------
• Any static web server (or just double-click the html file)  
• A modern browser that supports ES-modules (Chrome ≥ 61, Firefox ≥ 60, Edge ≥ 79, Safari ≥ 11)  
• An internet connection the first time (≈  350 MB are downloaded – afterwards the model is cached by the browser)  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
2.  index.html
--------------------------------------------------------------------
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Vanilla JS BlenderBot Chat</title>
    <style>
        /* ultra-simple styling — change to taste */
        *{box-sizing:border-box;font-family:system-ui,Arial;}
        body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;height:100vh;}
        #messages{flex:1;overflow:auto;padding:1rem;background:#f6f6f6;}
        .msg{max-width:75%;padding:.6rem 1rem;border-radius:.8rem;margin:.3rem 0;white-space:pre-wrap;line-height:1.4}
        .user     {align-self:flex-end;background:#2979ff;color:#fff;}
        .assistant{align-self:flex-start;background:#e0e0e0;color:#000;}
        #inputBar{display:flex;padding:.5rem;border-top:1px solid #ddd}
        #userInput{flex:1;padding:.4rem .6rem;font-size:1rem;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:.4rem}
        #sendBtn{margin-left:.5rem;padding:.4rem 1rem;font-size:1rem}
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div id="messages"></div>

    <div id="inputBar">
        <input  id="userInput" placeholder="Type a message and hit Enter…" autocomplete="off"/>
        <button id="sendBtn">Send</button>
    </div>

<!-- 1️⃣  Everything else lives in this ES-module script ---------------------->
<script type="module">
import { pipeline } from
    'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@xenova/transformers@2.7.0/dist/transformers.min.js';

/***********************************************************************
 * 0.  Tiny DOM helpers
 **********************************************************************/
const $messages = document.getElementById('messages');
const $input    = document.getElementById('userInput');
const $send     = document.getElementById('sendBtn');

function addMessage(role, text) {
    const div = document.createElement('div');
    div.className = `msg ${role === 'user' ? 'user' : 'assistant'}`;
    div.textContent = text;
    $messages.appendChild(div);
    $messages.scrollTop = $messages.scrollHeight;          // auto-scroll
}

/***********************************************************************
 * 1.  Load the model (runs once, takes a while the first time)
 **********************************************************************/
addMessage('assistant', '⏳ Loading facebook/blenderbot-400m-distill…');
const chat = await pipeline(
    'conversational',
    'facebook/blenderbot-400m-distill',
    { quantized: true }           // cuts the size roughly in half
);
$messages.lastChild.textContent = '✅ Model ready. Ask me anything!';

/***********************************************************************
 * 2.  Hold the conversation state so the bot remembers context
 **********************************************************************/
let conversation = null;          // will be filled after first turn

/***********************************************************************
 * 3.  Send handler
 **********************************************************************/
async function send() {
    const text = $input.value.trim();
    if (!text) return;

    addMessage('user', text);
    $input.value = '';
    $input.focus();

    // show a typing indicator
    const typing = document.createElement('div');
    typing.className = 'msg assistant';
    typing.textContent = '…';
    $messages.appendChild(typing);
    $messages.scrollTop = $messages.scrollHeight;

    /*************************************************************
     *  transformers.js API:
     *    result = await chat( message, { conversation } )
     *    result.generated_text         -> the assistant reply
     *    result.conversation           -> updated state object
     *************************************************************/
    try {
        const result = await chat(text, { conversation, temperature: 0.7 });
        conversation = result.conversation;     // keep the new state

        typing.textContent = result.generated_text.trim();
    } catch (err) {
        console.error(err);
        typing.textContent = '⚠️  Error: ' + err.message;
    }
}

/***********************************************************************
 * 4.  Wire UI events
 **********************************************************************/
$send.onclick = send;
$input.onkeydown = e => { if (e.key === 'Enter' && !e.shiftKey) { e.preventDefault(); send(); }};

</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Save the file, open it in a browser, wait until “Model ready” appears, and start chatting.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
3.  How it works
--------------------------------------------------------------------
1. The ES-module tag loads `@xenova/transformers` directly from jsDelivr.  
2. `pipeline('conversational', 'facebook/blenderbot-400m-distill')` downloads the ONNX weights from the Hugging Face Hub (≈ 350 MB quantized). The browser keeps them in IndexedDB, so the next page load is instant.  
3. The returned `chat` function expects a user string and optionally the previous `conversation` object. It sends both to Web-Assembly inference code executed fully client-side – no backend, no HF-token required.  
4. The object that comes back contains the generated answer and an updated conversation history, which we store and pass in the next call so BlenderBot has multi-turn memory.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
4.  Frequently asked questions
--------------------------------------------------------------------
“Isn’t 400 M too heavy for the browser?”  
  • It runs fine on desktop/laptop (≈ 2 – 3 GB RAM and WebGL). Mobile mostly works but will be slower.  
  • If you need something smaller, swap the model for e.g. `facebook/blenderbot_small-90M` or `microsoft/DialoGPT-medium`.  
––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  
“How do I deploy?”  
  • Any static host (GitHub Pages, Vercel static, Netlify, S3, etc.) – nothing server-side.  
––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  
“Can I stream tokens?”  
  • As of transformers.js v2.7 streaming is not yet exposed; generation is returned when finished. When streaming lands you can replace the typing placeholder with incremental updates.  
––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––  
“Can I run offline once the model is cached?”  
  • Yes. After the first successful load the model files are stored in browser storage; you can subsequently open the page with the network disabled.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
5.  Extending the demo
--------------------------------------------------------------------
• Replace the typing indicator with a spinner gif.  
• Add `max_new_tokens`, `temperature`, `top_p`, etc. sliders.  
• Store the message list in `localStorage` so the chat survives reloads.  
• Put each user into a separate `conversation_id` so you can share the same page with friends.  
• Use the Audio API + speech-to-text to create a fully voice-enabled assistant.

That is all you need: a single HTML file, transformers.js from a CDN, and the facebook/blenderbot-400m-distill model – pure vanilla JavaScript. Have fun!

Below is a completely-self-contained, single-file chess “shell” written in pure Python 3.  
It lets one human move the white pieces and then the black pieces (i.e. you play against yourself).  
It enforces the normal movement rules for every piece, disallows illegal moves, handles captures and pawn promotion to a queen, but omits the more advanced rules (castling, en-passant and the fifty-move/repetition rules).  

Save it as e.g.  self_chess.py and run with  python self_chess.py.  
Moves are entered in coordinate notation such as  e2e4  (from-square, to-square).  
Type  help  or  ?  while the program is running to see a mini command reference.

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Minimal self-play chess board.
Author: ChatGPT
"""

import sys

FILES = 'abcdefgh'
RANKS = '12345678'
EMPTY  = '.'

WHITE, BLACK = 'white', 'black'

def other(color):
    return WHITE if color == BLACK else BLACK


def algebraic_to_index(square: str):
    """'e4' -> (4, 3)  (# file, rank) with [0,7] = a1 .. h8"""
    if len(square) != 2 or square[0] not in FILES or square[1] not in RANKS:
        raise ValueError("Bad square name")
    file = FILES.index(square[0])
    rank = RANKS.index(square[1])
    return file, rank


def index_to_algebraic(file: int, rank: int):
    return FILES[file] + RANKS[rank]


class ChessGame:
    def __init__(self):
        self.board = self._starting_board()
        self.turn = WHITE
        self.fullmove = 1

    def _starting_board(self):
        """Return an 8×8 list (board[file][rank]) filled with piece letters or '.'"""
        b = [[EMPTY for _ in range(8)] for _ in range(8)]

        # place white pieces (uppercase)
        placement = ('RNBQKBNR', 'PPPPPPPP')   # rank 1, 2
        for f in range(8):
            b[f][0] = placement[0][f]
            b[f][1] = placement[1][f]

        # place black pieces (lowercase)
        placement = ('rnbqkbnr', 'pppppppp')   # rank 8, 7
        for f in range(8):
            b[f][7] = placement[0][f]
            b[f][6] = placement[1][f]
        return b

    # ────────────────────────── Display ────────────────────────── #

    def show(self):
        print("\n   +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
        for rank in reversed(range(8)):
            sys.stdout.write(f" {rank+1} |")
            for file in range(8):
                piece = self.board[file][rank]
                sys.stdout.write(f" {piece if piece != EMPTY else ' '} |")
            print("\n   +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
        print("     a   b   c   d   e   f   g   h ")
        print(f"Turn: {self.turn.capitalize()} (move {self.fullmove})\n")

    # ─────────────────────── Move interface ────────────────────── #

    def prompt_and_play(self):
        while True:
            self.show()
            move_text = input("Enter move (e2e4, 'help', 'quit'): ").strip().lower()
            if move_text in ('quit', 'exit'):
                print("Goodbye.")
                return
            if move_text in ('help', '?'):
                self._help()
                continue
            try:
                self.attempt_move(move_text)
                if self.turn == WHITE:               # every half-move, update full move counter
                    self.fullmove += 1
                self.turn = other(self.turn)
            except ValueError as exc:
                print(f"Illegal move: {exc}")

    def _help(self):
        print("Input a move in coordinate form:\n"
              "    e2e4   (from-square then to-square)\n"
              "    promotion: e7e8q (promote to queen/rook/bishop/knight)\n"
              "Other commands:\n"
              "    help  ?     show this\n"
              "    quit  exit  leave the program\n")

    # ────────────────────── Move validation ────────────────────── #

    def attempt_move(self, token: str):
        # Parse promotion
        promotion_piece = None
        if len(token) == 5:
            token, promo = token[:4], token[4]
            if promo not in 'qrbn':
                raise ValueError("promotion piece must be q, r, b or n")
            promotion_piece = promo

        if len(token) != 4:
            raise ValueError("move must be of the form e2e4")

        from_sq = token[:2]
        to_sq   = token[2:]

        fx, fy = algebraic_to_index(from_sq)
        tx, ty = algebraic_to_index(to_sq)

        piece = self.board[fx][fy]
        if piece == EMPTY:
            raise ValueError("no piece on from-square")
        if self._color_of_piece(piece) != self.turn:
            raise ValueError("that is not your piece")

        if not self._is_legal_move(piece, fx, fy, tx, ty):
            raise ValueError("piece cannot move that way")

        # If pawn reaches last rank, ensure promotion is supplied
        if piece.upper() == 'P' and (ty == 7 or ty == 0):
            if not promotion_piece:
                raise ValueError("pawn must promote (append q/r/b/n)")
            piece = promotion_piece.upper() if self.turn == WHITE else promotion_piece.lower()

        # Make the move
        self.board[tx][ty] = piece
        self.board[fx][fy] = EMPTY

    # ─────────────────── Piece specific legality ────────────────── #

    def _color_of_piece(self, piece):
        return WHITE if piece.isupper() else BLACK

    def _is_legal_move(self, piece, fx, fy, tx, ty):
        # Own piece on target?
        dest_piece = self.board[tx][ty]
        if dest_piece != EMPTY and self._color_of_piece(dest_piece) == self.turn:
            return False

        dx, dy = tx - fx, ty - fy
        adx, ady = abs(dx), abs(dy)

        piece_upper = piece.upper()

        if piece_upper == 'P':
            return self._pawn_legal(piece, fx, fy, tx, ty, dx, dy)
        if piece_upper == 'N':
            return (adx, ady) in ((1, 2), (2, 1))
        if piece_upper == 'B':
            if adx != ady: return False
            return self._clear_path(fx, fy, tx, ty)
        if piece_upper == 'R':
            if dx and dy: return False
            return self._clear_path(fx, fy, tx, ty)
        if piece_upper == 'Q':
            if dx and dy and adx != ady:  # not same file/rank/diagonal
                return False
            return self._clear_path(fx, fy, tx, ty)
        if piece_upper == 'K':
            return max(adx, ady) == 1   # castling omitted
        return False

    def _pawn_legal(self, piece, fx, fy, tx, ty, dx, dy):
        direction = 1 if piece.isupper() else -1   # white moves +1 rank
        start_rank = 1 if piece.isupper() else 6
        # Moving forward
        if dx == 0:
            # single square
            if dy == direction and self.board[tx][ty] == EMPTY:
                return True
            # double from start
            if fy == start_rank and dy == 2*direction and self.board[tx][ty] == EMPTY:
                between = fy + direction
                if self.board[fx][between] == EMPTY:
                    return True
            return False
        # Capturing
        if abs(dx) == 1 and dy == direction and self.board[tx][ty] != EMPTY:
            return True
        return False  # en-passant not implemented

    def _clear_path(self, fx, fy, tx, ty):
        """Return True if all squares between from and to are empty"""
        step_x = (tx - fx) and ((tx - fx) // abs(tx - fx))
        step_y = (ty - fy) and ((ty - fy) // abs(ty - fy))
        x, y = fx + step_x, fy + step_y
        while (x, y) != (tx, ty):
            if self.board[x][y] != EMPTY:
                return False
            x += step_x
            y += step_y
        return True


# ──────────────────────────── Main ─────────────────────────────── #

if __name__ == '__main__':
    game = ChessGame()
    try:
        game.prompt_and_play()
    except (KeyboardInterrupt, EOFError):
        print("\nBye.")
```

Key points & limitations
• Legal move checking for each piece, including path blocking for sliding pieces, pawn double pushes and promotions.  
• The program does NOT implement check / check-mate detection, castling, en-passant or draw rules. You are responsible for following those rules yourself while you play.  
• It is purposely minimal so you can read, hack and extend it easily.  

Enjoy playing (and modifying) your self-play chess program!

Below is a compact, self-contained example that shows one way to build a “radial slider” – a control that lets the visitor grow or shrink a circle by dragging a handle straight out from / back toward the centre.  
Everything is plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript (no frameworks), so you can copy the three snippets into one file and open it in a browser.

--------------------------------------------------
HTML
--------------------------------------------------
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Radial (Radius) Slider Demo</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>

<div id="radial-slider"
     data-min="20"    <!-- minimum radius  -->
     data-max="140"   <!-- maximum radius  -->
     data-step="10">  <!-- snap every … px -->

  <!-- SVG gives us circles that scale crisply -->
  <svg id="slider-svg" width="320" height="320">
      <g id="markers"></g>          <!-- concentric-ring marks -->
      <circle id="handle" r="8"/>   <!-- draggable knob -->
  </svg>

  <output id="radius-out">0</output> px
</div>

<script src="slider.js"></script>
</body>
</html>

--------------------------------------------------
CSS (style.css)
--------------------------------------------------
*{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif}

#radial-slider{
  width:320px;height:320px;         /* layout size                  */
  margin:3rem auto;display:block;
  position:relative;user-select:none
}

#slider-svg{width:100%;height:100%}

#markers circle{
  fill:none;
  stroke:#ccc;
  stroke-dasharray:4 4;
}

#handle{
  fill:#ff5722;
  stroke:#fff;
  stroke-width:2;
  cursor:pointer;
  pointer-events:all;
}

#radius-out{
  position:absolute;
  bottom:-2.5rem;
  left:50%;
  transform:translateX(-50%);
  font-size:1.25rem;
}

--------------------------------------------------
JavaScript (slider.js)
--------------------------------------------------
(() => {
  /* ───────────────────────── configuration ───────────────────────── */
  const slider   = document.getElementById('radial-slider');
  const svg      = document.getElementById('slider-svg');
  const markersG = document.getElementById('markers');
  const handle   = document.getElementById('handle');
  const readout  = document.getElementById('radius-out');

  const min  = +slider.dataset.min;   // numeric attribute values
  const max  = +slider.dataset.max;
  const step = +slider.dataset.step;

  const cx = svg.viewBox?.baseVal?.width/2 || svg.clientWidth/2;
  const cy = svg.viewBox?.baseVal?.height/2|| svg.clientHeight/2;

  /* ─────────────────────── draw concentric guides ─────────────────── */
  for (let r = min; r <= max; r += step) {
    const c = document.createElementNS('http://www.w3.org/2000/svg', 'circle');
    c.setAttribute('cx', cx);
    c.setAttribute('cy', cy);
    c.setAttribute('r',  r);
    markersG.appendChild(c);
  }

  /* ───────────────────────── helper functions ─────────────────────── */
  function clamp(val, lo, hi)   { return Math.max(lo, Math.min(hi, val)); }
  function snap(val, s)         { return Math.round(val / s) * s; }

  function updateHandleRadius(r) {
    // put knob on the horizontal (0°) by default
    handle.setAttribute('cx', cx + r);
    handle.setAttribute('cy', cy);
    readout.value = r;
  }

  /* ─────────────────────────── interaction ────────────────────────── */
  let dragging = false;

  function pointerDown(e) {
    dragging = true;
    svg.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
    pointerMove(e);              // update immediately
  }

  function pointerMove(e) {
    if (!dragging) return;
    const rect = svg.getBoundingClientRect();
    const x = e.clientX - rect.left;
    const y = e.clientY - rect.top;
    const dx = x - cx;
    const dy = y - cy;
    let r    = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
    r = snap(clamp(r, min, max), step);
    updateHandleRadius(r);
  }

  function pointerUp(e) {
    dragging = false;
    svg.releasePointerCapture(e.pointerId);
  }

  handle.addEventListener('pointerdown', pointerDown);
  svg   .addEventListener('pointermove', pointerMove);
  svg   .addEventListener('pointerup',   pointerUp);
  svg   .addEventListener('pointerleave',pointerUp);

  /* ─────────────────── initialise at minimum radius ────────────────── */
  updateHandleRadius(min);
})();

--------------------------------------------------
How it works
--------------------------------------------------
1. SVG canvas  
   - Origin centred (cx, cy) so concentric marker rings are easy: just <circle> elements with r growing in `step` increments from `data-min` to `data-max`.

2. Handle  
   - Another <circle>.  
   - Receives pointer events; on drag we measure mouse/finger distance from centre (`Math.hypot(dx,dy)`).

3. Value logic  
   - clamp → snap → update  
   - Value is clamped between min / max, then snapped to the desired step, then knob’s `cx` is set at `centreX + r` (0 ° for simplicity—you could project to the correct angle if you prefer free-angle dragging).

4. Output  
   - An <output> element under the control shows the current radius; you could also dispatch a `customEvent` so the rest of the page can react easily.

--------------------------------------------------
Possible extensions
--------------------------------------------------
• Let the user drag anywhere in the circle, not only the knob (just start drag on `svg`).  
• Add colour change / animation to emphasise growth.  
• Instead of snapping you could show interpolation by mapping radius to any property (opacity, width, etc.).  
• Wrap the logic in a class / Web Component or rewrite in React/Vue/… if you’re already using a framework.

This should get you started – drop the three blocks in a file, open it, and you have a radial slider that feels intuitive and mobile-friendly.

