I have part of a Javascript function that I want to rewrite. Currently it searches every property Matches to find the minimum, and makes Player2 always be the first member. Instead, I want Player1 to be the lowest result sorting by Matches, and Player2 to be random each time the code is run.

function elo(data) {
  // Find the two players with the fewest matches.
  let minMatches = Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER;
  let Player1 = null;
  let Player2 = null;
  for (let player of data) {
    if (player.Matches < minMatches) {
      minMatches = player.Matches;
      Player1 = player;
      Player2 = data.find(p => p !== Player1);
    }
  }
}

output:
Here’s one clean way to do it.  
• Player 1 → the player that has the fewest Matches.  
• Player 2 → a randomly chosen player from the remainder of the list.

```javascript
function elo(data) {
  if (!Array.isArray(data) || data.length < 2) {
    throw new Error('Need at least two players');
  }

  // 1. Find the player with the fewest matches
  const Player1 = data.reduce(
    (minSoFar, p) => (p.Matches < minSoFar.Matches ? p : minSoFar)
  );

  // 2. Pick a random opponent that isn't Player1
  const others  = data.filter(p => p !== Player1);
  const Player2 = others[Math.floor(Math.random() * others.length)];

  return { Player1, Player2 };
}
```

• `reduce` walks the array once to locate the minimum `Matches` value.  
• The `filter` removes that minimum player, and `Math.random()` selects a different player each time the function is called.

```javascript
/*  F(n) (mod p) with O(log n) time and O(1) memory
    – fast-doubling formulae
       F(2k)     = F(k)·[2·F(k+1) – F(k)]
       F(2k + 1) = F(k)^2 + F(k+1)^2
*/
function fibMod (n, p) {
    if (p === 1) return 0;                    // everything is 0 (mod 1)

    const MOD = BigInt(p);                    // let JS do the bignum arithmetics
    const N   = BigInt(n);

    // Returns [ F(k) (mod MOD),  F(k+1) (mod MOD) ]
    function pair (k) {
        if (k === 0n) return [0n, 1n];

        const [fk, fk1] = pair(k >> 1n);      // k // 2
        const t1 = (2n * fk1 - fk + MOD) % MOD;
        const c  = (fk * t1) % MOD;           // F(2k)
        const d  = (fk * fk + fk1 * fk1) % MOD;   // F(2k+1)

        return (k & 1n)                       // k odd?
               ? [d, (c + d) % MOD]           //   [F(2k+1), F(2k+2)]
               : [c, d];                      //   [F(2k),   F(2k+1)]
    }

    return Number(pair(N)[0]);                // first element is F(n) mod p
}

/* --------------  examples -------------- */
console.log(fibMod(10,    1000));     // 55
console.log(fibMod(1000,  1000000007)); // 517691607
console.log(fibMod(1e18,  97));       // works for very large n
```

Why it works
1. ​Fast-doubling computes `F(k)` and `F(k+1)` from the same pair for `⌊k/2⌋`, so only `O(log n)` recursive levels are needed.
2. All operations are performed as `BigInt`, then reduced modulo `p`, so there is no overflow.
3. Only two `BigInt`s are kept at any moment – constant extra memory.

If you prefer an all-`BigInt` result, just drop the final `Number( … )` cast.

Below is a compact, synthesis-safe SystemVerilog example that constrains a 32-bit random value so that exactly five bit positions toggle with respect to the previously generated value (i.e. the Hamming distance between the two words is 5).

```systemverilog
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// File : five_transitions.sv
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
class five_transition_c;

   // ------------------------------------------------------------
   // Data
   // ------------------------------------------------------------
   rand  bit [31:0]  curr;             // value to be randomized
         bit [31:0]  prev = '0;        // previous value (not random)

   // ------------------------------------------------------------
   // Constraint : exactly 5 bit positions must toggle
   // ------------------------------------------------------------
   //
   // 1.  prev ^ curr  -> 1 where the two words differ
   // 2.  $countones() -> population count of that XOR
   //
   constraint five_toggle_c {
      $countones( curr ^ prev ) == 5;
   }

   // ------------------------------------------------------------
   // Keep prev updated for the next randomization cycle
   // ------------------------------------------------------------
   function void post_randomize();
      prev = curr;
   endfunction

endclass

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Simple testbench to show it works
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
module tb;
   five_transition_c gen = new;

   initial begin
      repeat (10) begin
         void'( gen.randomize() );
         $display("curr = %08x  prev = %08x  transitions = %0d",
                  gen.curr, gen.prev,
                  $countones(gen.curr ^ gen.prev) );
      end
   end
endmodule
```

Key points

• `$countones()` is an IEEE-1800 SystemVerilog system function (supported by the major simulators) that returns the number of set bits in its argument.  
• The XOR (`^`) between the current and previous word produces a 1 wherever the two bits differ, so counting those 1’s gives the number of transitions.  
• `prev` is declared as a normal (non-rand) variable; it gets updated in `post_randomize()` so that each new randomization run compares the fresh word against the word produced in the preceding call.  
• The first call starts from 32'h0 (or any value you prefer) because `prev` is initialized to zero.

If your tool chain does not yet support `$countones()`, you can replace it with a small pure function:

```systemverilog
function automatic int popcount32 (bit [31:0] x);
   int cnt = 0;
   for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) cnt += x[i];
   return cnt;
endfunction

constraint five_toggle_c {
   popcount32( curr ^ prev ) == 5;
}
```

Either form guarantees that every newly generated 32-bit word differs from the previous one in exactly five bit positions.

Below is a single-file “whole-program” example that can be compiled with OCaml ≥ 4.14, the
ocaml-gl bindings (https://github.com/ocamllabs/ocaml-gl), and Tsdl for the OpenGL context.
It shows:

• creating a GL context with SDL2  
• loading and compiling a compute shader written in GLSL 4.30  
• uploading two single-precision vectors (A and B) to Shader-Storage-Buffer-Objects  
• executing the shader to obtain Σᵢ Aᵢ · Bᵢ  
• reading the single‐float result back to the CPU and printing it.

===========================================================================  
File : compute_dot.ml
===========================================================================

```ocaml
(* compute_dot.ml *)

open Bigarray

(* ---------- tiny helpers ------------------------------------------------ *)

let check status get_info log_fn obj kind =
  if status = 0 then begin
    Printf.eprintf "%s error:\n%s\n%!" kind (get_info obj);
    log_fn ();
    exit 1
  end

let ( ++ ) = Int.add                  (* just for readability *)

(* ---------- SDL2 / OpenGL initialisation -------------------------------- *)

let init_context () =
  ignore (Tsdl.Sdl.init Tsdl.Sdl.Init.video);
  (* core 4.3 context – we need compute shaders                       *)
  ignore (Tsdl.Sdl.gl_set_attribute Tsdl.Sdl.Gl.context_major_version 4);
  ignore (Tsdl.Sdl.gl_set_attribute Tsdl.Sdl.Gl.context_minor_version 3);
  ignore (Tsdl.Sdl.gl_set_attribute Tsdl.Sdl.Gl.context_profile_mask
            Tsdl.Sdl.Gl.context_profile_core);

  let w =
    Tsdl.Sdl.create_window
      ~w:640 ~h:480 "Compute-only"
      Tsdl.Sdl.(Window.opengl ++ Window.hidden)
  in
  ignore (Tsdl.Sdl.gl_create_context w);
  Gl.init ();                 (* load OpenGL entry points            *)
  w                            (* keep window alive → keeps context  *)

(* ---------- GLSL compute shader ---------------------------------------- *)

let dot_cs_src = {|
#version 430
layout(local_size_x = 256) in;

/* A and B are the input vectors, binding points 0 and 1              */
layout(std430, binding = 0) readonly buffer A { float a[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 1) readonly buffer B { float b[]; };

/* binding 2 holds one float (the accumulated result)                 */
layout(std430, binding = 2) writeonly buffer R { float r[]; };

shared float partial[256];   // per-work-group scratch pad

void main()
{
    uint gid  = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;        // global element
    uint lid  = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;         // 0-255

    /* element-wise product stored in shared memory                   */
    partial[lid] = a[gid] * b[gid];
    barrier();                                 // wait for all threads

    /* one thread per group performs a reduction into r[0]            */
    if (lid == 0) {
        float s = 0.0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 256; ++i)
            s += partial[i];
        atomicAdd(r[0], s);                    // accumulate
    }
}
|}

(* ---------- OpenGL helper primitives ----------------------------------- *)

let compile_compute_shader src =
  let sid = Gl.create_shader Gl.compute_shader in
  Gl.shader_source sid src;
  Gl.compile_shader sid;
  let ok =
    let a = Array1.create int32 C_layout 1 in
    Gl.get_shaderiv sid Gl.compile_status a;  Int32.to_int a.{0}
  in
  check ok
    (fun s -> Gl.get_shader_info_log s)
    (fun () -> ())
    sid "shader compilation";

  let pid = Gl.create_program () in
  Gl.attach_shader pid sid;
  Gl.link_program pid;
  let linked =
    let a = Array1.create int32 C_layout 1 in
    Gl.get_programiv pid Gl.link_status a;  Int32.to_int a.{0}
  in
  check linked
    (fun p -> Gl.get_program_info_log p)
    (fun () -> ())
    pid "program link";
  pid

(* ---------- Buffer utilities ------------------------------------------- *)

let create_ssbo ~binding ~bytes ?data () =
  let id = Gl.gen_buffer () in
  Gl.bind_buffer_base Gl.shader_storage_buffer binding id;
  Gl.bind_buffer Gl.shader_storage_buffer id;
  Gl.buffer_data Gl.shader_storage_buffer bytes data Gl.static_draw;
  id

(* ---------- Main ------------------------------------------------------- *)

let () =
  let _window = init_context () in

  (* number of elements; must be multiple of 256 for the simplistic shader *)
  let n = 1 lsl 10 (* 1024 *) in
  let float_size = 4 in

  (* create and fill two OCaml bigarrays *)
  let make_vec f =
    let v = Array1.create float32 C_layout n in
    for i = 0 to n - 1 do v.{i} <- f i done; v
  in
  let a = make_vec (fun i -> float i) in
  let b = make_vec (fun _ -> 1.0)   in        (* dot product should be Σ i   *)

  (* result container (single float) *)
  let r = Array1.create float32 C_layout 1 in
  r.{0} <- 0.0;

  (* upload to OpenGL *)
  let _ba = create_ssbo ~binding:0 ~bytes:(n * float_size)
              ~data:(Some (Gl.bigarray_start Gl.array1 a)) () in
  let _bb = create_ssbo ~binding:1 ~bytes:(n * float_size)
              ~data:(Some (Gl.bigarray_start Gl.array1 b)) () in
  let br  = create_ssbo ~binding:2 ~bytes:float_size
              ~data:(Some (Gl.bigarray_start Gl.array1 r)) () in

  (* build program and run ------------------------------------------------ *)
  let prog = compile_compute_shader dot_cs_src in
  Gl.use_program prog;

  let groups = (n + 255) / 256 in       (* 256 threads per WG             *)
  Gl.dispatch_compute groups 1 1;
  Gl.memory_barrier Gl.shader_storage_barrier_bit;

  (* read back the single float from the R SSBO -------------------------- *)
  Gl.bind_buffer Gl.shader_storage_buffer br;
  let mapped =
    Gl.map_buffer_range Gl.shader_storage_buffer 0 float_size
      [Gl.map_read_bit]
  in
  let res =
    (Obj.magic mapped
       : (float, float32_elt, c_layout) Array1.t).{0}
  in
  Printf.printf "GPU dot product = %.0f (expected = %.0f)\n%!"
    res (Array1.fold_left (+.) 0.0 a);

  (* tidy up ------------------------------------------------------------- *)
  Gl.unmap_buffer Gl.shader_storage_buffer;
  Tsdl.Sdl.quit ()
```

===========================================================================  
Compiling and running
===========================================================================

1. Install dependencies (example with opam):

```
opam install tsdl tsdl-image ocaml-gl dune
```

2. `dune` file:

```
(executable
 (name compute_dot)
 (libraries tsdl gl))
```

3. Build & run:

```
dune exec ./compute_dot.exe
```

Typical output:

```
GPU dot product = 523776 (expected = 523776)
```

(the sum of 0 + 1 + … + 1023 equals 523 776).

The program contains no rendering—only the compute invocation—so the SDL
window never becomes visible; it merely provides the required OpenGL 4.3
context for the compute shader to run.

Below is one possible “classic Wolfenstein-style” ray-caster written completely in GLSL.  
The C / host side only has to draw a full-screen triangle (or quad) while supplying a few uniforms:

• a 2-D grid map stored in a small R-channel texture (value 0 = empty cell, value 1…n = wall id)  
• a texture atlas that keeps all wall textures next to each other horizontally  
• player position, view direction and camera-plane (camera-plane = dir rotated 90° and scaled so that |plane| = tan(fov/2))  
• viewport resolution

-----------------------------------------------------------------
Vertex shader  (fullscreen quad)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
#version 330 core

layout(location = 0) in vec2 aPos;   // ‑1 … +1

out vec2 vUV;

void main()
{
    vUV        = aPos * 0.5 + 0.5;   // 0 … 1, not really needed but handy
    gl_Position = vec4(aPos, 0.0, 1.0);
}

-----------------------------------------------------------------
Fragment shader  (old-school ray-casting)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
#version 330 core

in  vec2 vUV;
out vec4 fragColor;

/* --- Map and textures ---------------------------------------------------- */
uniform sampler2D uMap;       // tiny texture, one texel = one map cell
uniform int       uMapSize;   // width = height of the map texture
uniform sampler2D uAtlas;     // wall textures side-by-side
uniform int       uAtlasCols; // number of tiles inside the atlas (x-direction)

/* --- Player / camera ----------------------------------------------------- */
uniform vec2 uPlayerPos;      // in map space
uniform vec2 uPlayerDir;      // must be normalized
uniform vec2 uCameraPlane;    // perpendicular to Dir, controls FOV

/* --- Misc. --------------------------------------------------------------- */
uniform vec2 uResolution;     // window size in pixels

const int MAX_STEPS = 128;    // DDA safety cap

/* helper: fetch the wall id found at integer map-coordinate ‘p’ */
float mapSample(vec2 p)
{
    // Add 0.5 so that integer coords hit texel centres.
    return texture(uMap, (p + 0.5) / float(uMapSize)).r;
}

void main()
{
    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 1. build ray direction for this fragment                       */
    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    vec2 fragCoord = gl_FragCoord.xy;

    // screenX ∊ [-1 … 1]
    float screenX = (fragCoord.x / uResolution.x) * 2.0 - 1.0;
    vec2  rayDir  = normalize(uPlayerDir + screenX * uCameraPlane);

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 2. initialise DDA                                              */
    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    vec2 mapPos = floor(uPlayerPos);      // which square are we in?

    vec2 deltaDist = abs(vec2(1.0) / rayDir);
    vec2 sideDist;
    vec2 step;

    if (rayDir.x < 0.0) {
        step.x     = -1.0;
        sideDist.x = (uPlayerPos.x - mapPos.x) * deltaDist.x;
    } else {
        step.x     =  1.0;
        sideDist.x = (mapPos.x + 1.0 - uPlayerPos.x) * deltaDist.x;
    }
    if (rayDir.y < 0.0) {
        step.y     = -1.0;
        sideDist.y = (uPlayerPos.y - mapPos.y) * deltaDist.y;
    } else {
        step.y     =  1.0;
        sideDist.y = (mapPos.y + 1.0 - uPlayerPos.y) * deltaDist.y;
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 3. DDA walk through the grid                                   */
    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    int  hit  = 0;
    int  side = 0;                 // 0 = hit an x-side, 1 = y-side

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_STEPS && hit == 0; ++i)
    {
        if (sideDist.x < sideDist.y) {
            sideDist.x += deltaDist.x;
            mapPos.x   += step.x;
            side        = 0;
        } else {
            sideDist.y += deltaDist.y;
            mapPos.y   += step.y;
            side        = 1;
        }

        if (mapSample(mapPos) > 0.0)   // non-zero cell means wall hit
            hit = 1;
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 4. Nothing hit?  Draw sky/floor only                           */
    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    if (hit == 0)
    {
        fragColor = vec4(0.5, 0.7, 1.0, 1.0);                  // sky blue
        return;
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 5. perpendicular distance  (remove fish-eye)                   */
    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    float perpDist =
        (side == 0)
        ? (mapPos.x - uPlayerPos.x + (1.0 - step.x) * 0.5) / rayDir.x
        : (mapPos.y - uPlayerPos.y + (1.0 - step.y) * 0.5) / rayDir.y;

    /* vertical slice height in pixel space */
    float lineHeight = uResolution.y / perpDist;

    float drawStart = -lineHeight * 0.5 + uResolution.y * 0.5;
    float drawEnd   =  lineHeight * 0.5 + uResolution.y * 0.5;

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 6. If this fragment’s y belongs to ceiling or floor, draw that */
    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    if (fragCoord.y < drawStart)           // ceiling
    {
        fragColor = vec4(0.30, 0.30, 0.35, 1.0);
        return;
    }
    if (fragCoord.y > drawEnd)             // floor
    {
        fragColor = vec4(0.20, 0.15, 0.10, 1.0);
        return;
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* 7. Wall texture coordinates                                    */
    /* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    float wallX = (side == 0)
                ? (uPlayerPos.y + perpDist * rayDir.y)
                : (uPlayerPos.x + perpDist * rayDir.x);
    wallX -= floor(wallX);                 // 0 … 1 across the wall

    /* Which texture in the atlas?  (id is stored in map’s red channel) */
    int   texID          = int(mapSample(mapPos) * 255.0 + 0.5);
    float atlasTileWidth = 1.0 / float(uAtlasCols);

    vec2  texCoord;
    texCoord.x = (float(texID) + wallX) * atlasTileWidth;
    texCoord.y = (fragCoord.y - drawStart) / (drawEnd - drawStart);

    vec4 texColor = texture(uAtlas, texCoord);

    /* cheap shading: darken y-sides to fake light */
    if (side == 1)
        texColor.rgb *= 0.7;

    fragColor = texColor;
}

-----------------------------------------------------------------
How to use from the CPU side (pseudo):
-----------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. build a tiny (say 64×64) R8 texture that contains the map
//    each texel: 0 = empty, 1,2,3 … = different wall textures
// 2. build a 1×N atlas holding the wall textures side by side
// 3. send the following uniforms every frame:
glUniform2f(uPlayerPos,      player.x, player.y);
glUniform2f(uPlayerDir,      dir.x,    dir.y);
glUniform2f(uCameraPlane,    plane.x,  plane.y);
glUniform2f(uResolution,     winW,     winH);
glUniform1i(uMapSize,        mapSize);
glUniform1i(uAtlasCols,      numTiles);

// finally draw one full-screen triangle with the above shaders.

The shader reproduces the exact integer-DDA logic of early 90-ies software engines, but everything happens per-fragment on the GPU, giving a faithful “old-school” ray-casting look while costing basically nothing on modern hardware.

Below is a small, robust utility that will give you every displayable character that is actually on-screen in an HTML document, while automatically skipping the kinds of nodes that normally shouldn’t be counted as “text” (script, style, noscript, iframe, canvas, etc.).

```js
/**
 * getDocumentText
 * ---------------
 * Returns a plain-text representation of an HTML (or XHTML) document.
 *
 *  • Runs in any modern browser.
 *  • Optionally accepts a DOM Document or Element to work on;
 *    defaults to the global `document`.
 *  • Uses a TreeWalker so it’s fast even on big pages.
 *  • Discards empty/whitespace-only nodes and anything that lives
 *    inside tags such as <script>, <style>, <canvas>, <svg> …
 *  • Each text node is terminated with “\n” so paragraphs stay separated.
 */
function getDocumentText(root = document) {
  // Choose the subtree’s root.  If someone passed a full document,
  // take its body—otherwise treat the given node itself as the root.
  const startNode = root.body || root;

  // All tag names whose textual descendants we want to ignore.
  const SKIP_PARENTS = new Set([
    'SCRIPT', 'STYLE', 'NOSCRIPT', 'IFRAME',
    'CANVAS', 'SVG', 'AUDIO', 'VIDEO', 'PICTURE'
  ]);

  // Create the walker: we only want TEXT_NODEs.
  const walker = document.createTreeWalker(
    startNode,
    NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT,
    {
      acceptNode(node) {
        // Don’t include empty strings or whitespace-only nodes.
        if (!node.nodeValue || !node.nodeValue.trim()) {
          return NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT;
        }
        // Ignore text that lives inside black-listed elements.
        const parentName = node.parentNode && node.parentNode.nodeName;
        return SKIP_PARENTS.has(parentName)
          ? NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT
          : NodeFilter.FILTER_ACCEPT;
      }
    }
  );

  // Walk the tree and build the result.
  let buffer = [];
  while (walker.nextNode()) {
    buffer.push(walker.currentNode.nodeValue.trim());
  }

  // Join with newlines so sentences/paragraphs stay separated.
  return buffer.join('\n');
}

/* -----------------------------------------------------
   Example usage
   ----------------------------------------------------- */

// At any point inside your page / content-script / dev-tools …
console.log(getDocumentText());

// Extract just a fragment’s text:
const sidebar = document.querySelector('#sidebar');
console.log(getDocumentText(sidebar));

```

Why not simply use `document.body.innerText`?

• `innerText` forces a layout and re-flow, making it noticeably slower on large pages.  
• It still includes the contents of certain hidden elements.  
• You have no opportunity to alter the accept/reject rules.

Using `TreeWalker` avoids those drawbacks and gives you fine-grained control (e.g., add “MATH” if MathML text should be ignored, or delete “SVG” if you actually want the `<text>` inside an `<svg>`).

Drop the function in any browser-side JavaScript and you’ve got a reliable way to turn a rendered document into plain text.

Here is a concise, generic helper that uses the Stream API to fulfil the three requested cases:

1. If the list contains more than one element → throw an exception  
2. If the list contains exactly one element → return that element  
3. If the list is empty → return `null`

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public final class StreamHelpers {

    /**
     * Returns the single element of the list, {@code null} when the list is empty, 
     * and throws an {@link IllegalStateException} when the list contains more than one element.
     */
    public static <T> T singleOrNull(List<T> list) {
        return list.stream()
                   // We only need to look at the first two items to decide
                   .limit(2)
                   .collect(Collectors.collectingAndThen(Collectors.toList(), items -> {
                       if (items.size() > 1) {
                           throw new IllegalStateException("List contains more than one element");
                       }
                       return items.isEmpty() ? null : items.get(0);
                   }));
    }

    // ─── Example usage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(singleOrNull(List.of()));                 // → null
        System.out.println(singleOrNull(List.of("only")));           // → only
        System.out.println(singleOrNull(List.of("a", "b")));         // → IllegalStateException
    }
}
```

Explanation of the stream pipeline:

• `limit(2)` – we are only interested in the first two elements; if two are found we know the list is “too long”.  
• `collect(Collectors.toList())` – puts (at most) those two items in a list.  
• `collectingAndThen(..., items -> { ... })` – post-processes the collected list:  
  – size `> 1` ⭢ throw exception  
  – size `== 1` ⭢ return the single element  
  – size `== 0` ⭢ return `null`.

The method is generic (`<T>`) and works for any element type.

Below is a minimal, end-to-end example that

• captures the microphone  
• sends the PCM data to C++ through JNI  
• adds a simple Schroeder-type reverb  
• plays the processed signal back in real time.

The code is **not production ready** (no denormal protection, no latency measurement, error checks shortened, etc.) but it is fully self-contained and shows every step that is usually hard to piece together.

