Below is the quickest-to-understand pattern for an “IP-whitelist” middleware in Laravel.  
It is 100 % framework-native—no third-party packages—and can be adapted in a minute or two.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1.  Make the middleware
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
php artisan make:middleware CheckIp

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2.  Implement the check
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
app/Http/Middleware/CheckIp.php

<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;

class CheckIp
{
    /**
     * Addresses that are allowed to enter the application.
     * Tip: move this to a config file or .env if you don’t want to
     *      re-deploy just to change the list.
     */
    protected array $allowed = [
        '127.0.0.1',  // localhost (IPv4)
        '::1',        // localhost (IPv6)
        '203.0.113.42',
        '198.51.100.0/24', // an example CIDR range (see note below)
    ];

    public function handle($request, Closure $next)
    {
        // Grab the client address resolved by Laravel’s TrustProxies middleware
        $ip = $request->ip();          // returns single IP
        $ips = $request->ips();        // returns array if “X-Forwarded-For” present

        if (!$this->isAllowed($ip, $ips)) {
            // Stop the request right here
            abort(Response::HTTP_FORBIDDEN, 'Your IP address is not authorised.');
        }

        // All good – continue the request
        return $next($request);
    }

    /**
     * Decide if at least one address is whitelisted.
     */
    protected function isAllowed(string $ip, array $ips): bool
    {
        // 1. direct match
        if (in_array($ip, $this->allowed, true)) {
            return true;
        }

        // 2. match any forwarded address
        foreach ($ips as $fwd) {
            if (in_array($fwd, $this->allowed, true)) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        // 3. support simple CIDR notation (optional)
        foreach ($this->allowed as $allowed) {
            if (str_contains($allowed, '/')
                && $this->cidrMatch($ip, $allowed)) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    /**
     * Minimal CIDR check (for IPv4 only).  Remove if you don’t need it.
     */
    protected function cidrMatch(string $ip, string $cidr): bool
    {
        [$subnet, $mask] = explode('/', $cidr);
        return (ip2long($ip) & ~((1 << (32 - $mask)) - 1))
                === ip2long($subnet);
    }
}

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3.  Register the middleware
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
app/Http/Kernel.php

protected $routeMiddleware = [
    // …
    'ip.whitelist' => \App\Http\Middleware\CheckIp::class,
];

(If you want the rule to apply to every request, add it to the $middleware array instead.)

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4.  Attach it to routes
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Single route
Route::get('/admin', 'AdminController@index')->middleware('ip.whitelist');

// Or an entire group
Route::middleware('ip.whitelist')->group(function () {
    // …protected routes here…
});

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Optional improvements
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
•   Config-driven list  
    Create config/whitelist.php

    return [
        'ips' => explode(',', env('ALLOWED_IPS', '127.0.0.1')),
    ];

    Then replace $this->allowed with config('whitelist.ips').

•   Logging / monitoring  
    Instead of abort() you could Log::warning() first or fire an event.

•   Reverse-proxy awareness  
    Ensure App\Http\Middleware\TrustProxies is configured so that $request->ip() reflects the real client IP when behind Cloudflare / ELB, etc.

That’s all that is required: a <50-line class, a registration line in Kernel, and a single middleware declaration on the routes you want protected.

