The snippet is a very common pattern used to build a “multi-step” (wizard-style) HTML form. Each visual screen of the wizard is a DOM element with the class .step; at any moment one of them also carries the class .active so that only that screen is shown (typically via CSS such as .step{display:none}.step.active{display:block}).  
Below is a walk-through of what every statement does.

1. Gathering references
   • const steps = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll("form .step"));
     – querySelectorAll returns a NodeList containing every descendant of <form> that has the class step.  
     – Array.from converts that NodeList into a real Array, giving us array methods such as indexOf.

   • const nextBtn  = document.querySelectorAll("form .next-btn");
   • const prevBtn  = document.querySelectorAll("form .previous-btn");
     – NodeLists of all “Next” and “Previous” buttons inside the form.

   • const form = document.querySelector("form");
     – Reference to the first <form> element in the document.

2. “Next” button click handler
   nextBtn.forEach((button) => {
       button.addEventListener("click", () => {
           changeStep("next");
       });
   });
   – For every button with class .next-btn attach a click listener that simply calls changeStep with the argument "next".

3. “Previous” button click handler
   prevBtn.forEach((button) => {
       button.addEventListener("click", () => {
           changeStep("prev");
       });
   });
   – Same idea, but passes "prev".

4. Submit handler for the whole form
   form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
       e.preventDefault();           // stop normal form submission / page reload
       const inputs = [];
       form.querySelectorAll("input").forEach((input) => {
           const { name, value } = input; // destructuring
           inputs.push({ name, value });  // collect key/value pairs
       });
       console.log(inputs);              // imitate “send to server”
       form.reset();                     // clear the form (takes it back to its initial values)
   });
   – When the user eventually presses <button type="submit"> the code:
     • Stops the browser’s default POST/GET action.
     • Loops over every <input> inside the form, collects each field’s name and value into an array of objects.
     • Logs that array (placeholder for AJAX, etc.).
     • Resets the form so it’s ready to be filled again.

5. changeStep helper
   function changeStep(btn) {
       let index = 0;
       const active = document.querySelector(".active"); // the currently visible panel
       index = steps.indexOf(active);                    // its position in the steps array
       steps[index].classList.remove("active");          // hide current step

       if (btn === "next") {
           index++;      // move forward
       } else if (btn === "prev") {
           index--;      // move backward
       }

       steps[index].classList.add("active");             // show the new step
   }
   – Receives a direction string ("next" or "prev").
   – Finds the currently active step, removes .active from it.
   – Increments or decrements the index.
   – Adds .active to the new step, which causes the next/previous panel to become visible.

Important details / caveats
• No bounds checking is performed—clicking “Previous” on the first page or “Next” on the last would throw an error (`steps[index]` would be undefined). This could be fixed with guards like:
    if (btn === "next" && index < steps.length - 1) index++;
• Only <input> elements are collected on submit; selects, textareas, etc. would require additional selectors.
• The code assumes there is always exactly one element with class .active at any given time.

In short:  
– “Next” and “Previous” buttons call changeStep to swap which .step element has the .active class.  
– The submit listener gathers form data manually, prevents the default HTTP submission, prints the data, and resets the form.

