HighFive needs to know the layout of every user defined type before it can create a data-set.  
The easiest way to describe the layout is to use the helper macro that HighFive
ships with.

```cpp
#include <highfive/H5File.hpp>
#include <highfive/H5CompoundType.hpp>

struct Data {
    double v[3];   // three doubles
    int    n;      // one integer
};

// Tell HighFive how the structure looks like
HIGHFIVE_REGISTER_TYPE(Data, v, n)     // <─ that is all you need

int main()
{
    HighFive::File file("test.h5", HighFive::File::Overwrite);

    std::vector<Data> buffer(10);
    /* fill buffer … */

    auto dset = file.createDataSet("data", HighFive::DataSpace::From(buffer));
    dset.write(buffer);                 // HighFive automatically uses the
                                        // compound datatype we just registered
}
```

The macro expands to something equivalent to

```cpp
namespace HighFive {
template<>
struct CompoundType<Data> {
    static CompoundType create() {
        // ----- type of member “v” : fixed length array -----------------
        hsize_t dims[] = {3};
        DataType v_type = ArrayType(AtomicType<double>(), 1, dims);

        // ----- build the compound type ---------------------------------
        CompoundType ct(sizeof(Data));
        ct.insertMember("v", HOFFSET(Data, v), v_type);
        ct.insertMember("n", HOFFSET(Data, n), AtomicType<int>());
        return ct;
    }
};
}
```

So, either use `HIGHFIVE_REGISTER_TYPE(Data, v, n)` (recommended) or write the
`CompoundType<Data>` specialisation manually as shown above—both approaches
create the correct compound HDF5 datatype for

```cpp
struct Data { double v[3]; int n; };
```

Below is a complete, annotated walkthrough that takes you from an empty directory to a working “baby-grep” implemented in safe, idiomatic Rust.  
The program:

• is single-file (main.rs)  
• takes a literal substring to look for (no regex)  
• accepts one or more files, or - for stdin  
• prints every matching line

I assume you already have rustup / cargo installed.  
If not, get it at https://rustup.rs first.

────────────────────────
1. Create the project
────────────────────────
$ cargo new babygrep        # makes ./babygrep
$ cd babygrep

We’ll keep dependencies at zero for now; std is enough.

────────────────────────
2. Skeleton main.rs
────────────────────────
Delete the “hello world” and start with the minimum that compiles:

```rust
// src/main.rs
fn main() {
    println!("stub");
}
```

$ cargo run                        # sanity check

────────────────────────
3. Decide the public contract
────────────────────────
CLI syntax (loosely following traditional grep):

    babygrep <needle> <file ..>        # read each file
    babygrep <needle> -                # read stdin
    babygrep <needle>                  # read stdin (implicit -)

Therefore we need:

• pattern  : String (owned, cheap to clone when short)  
• files    : Vec<String>              

────────────────────────
4. Parse CLI arguments
────────────────────────
Rust’s std::env::args() is fine for this simple use-case.  
Experienced dev tip: turn argument parsing into a small builder so you get
Result-based error handling and the borrow checker stays quiet.

```rust
use std::env;

struct Config {
    pattern: String,
    files: Vec<String>,
}

impl Config {
    fn build(mut args: impl Iterator<Item = String>) -> Result<Self, &'static str> {
        let _exe_name = args.next();                // skip argv[0]

        let pattern = args.next().ok_or("missing <needle> pattern")?;
        let files: Vec<String> = args.collect();

        // Implicit stdin if no file provided
        let files = if files.is_empty() {
            vec!["-".to_string()]
        } else {
            files
        };

        Ok(Self { pattern, files })
    }
}
```

────────────────────────
5. IO helpers
────────────────────────
We want a single code path that can read either a file or stdin.  
In Rust you can trait-object-erase BufRead:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{self, BufRead, BufReader};

fn open_buf(path: &str) -> Result<Box<dyn BufRead>, io::Error> {
    if path == "-" {
        Ok(Box::new(BufReader::new(io::stdin())))
    } else {
        let file = File::open(path)?;
        Ok(Box::new(BufReader::new(file)))
    }
}
```

────────────────────────
6. The search routine
────────────────────────
String::contains does exactly what we want and works on &str slices.

```rust
fn search_lines<R: BufRead>(reader: R, needle: &str) -> io::Result<()> {
    for line in reader.lines() {
        let line = line?;                   // propagate IO errors
        if line.contains(needle) {
            println!("{line}");
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

────────────────────────
7. The orchestration (run)
────────────────────────
Let’s separate run() so that main() stays tiny and we can bubble up
errors with the ? operator.

```rust
use std::error::Error;

fn run(cfg: Config) -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    for path in cfg.files {
        let reader = open_buf(&path)?;
        search_lines(reader, &cfg.pattern)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

────────────────────────
8. Wiring it all together
────────────────────────
Now main() is just bootstrap + report errors:

```rust
fn main() {
    if let Err(e) = run_app() {
        eprintln!("error: {e}");
        std::process::exit(1);
    }
}

fn run_app() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let cfg = Config::build(env::args())?;
    run(cfg)
}
```

────────────────────────
9. The complete file
────────────────────────
Putting every snippet together:

```rust
// src/main.rs
use std::env;
use std::error::Error;
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{self, BufRead, BufReader};

fn main() {
    if let Err(e) = run_app() {
        eprintln!("error: {e}");
        std::process::exit(1);
    }
}

fn run_app() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    let cfg = Config::build(env::args())?;
    run(cfg)
}

/* ---------- CLI CONFIG ------------ */

struct Config {
    pattern: String,
    files: Vec<String>,
}

impl Config {
    fn build(mut args: impl Iterator<Item = String>) -> Result<Self, &'static str> {
        let _exe_name = args.next();
        let pattern = args.next().ok_or("missing <needle>")?;
        let files: Vec<String> = args.collect();
        let files = if files.is_empty() { vec!["-".into()] } else { files };
        Ok(Self { pattern, files })
    }
}

/* ---------- IO + SEARCH ------------ */

fn run(cfg: Config) -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    for path in cfg.files {
        let reader = open_buf(&path)?;
        search_lines(reader, &cfg.pattern)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}

fn open_buf(path: &str) -> Result<Box<dyn BufRead>, io::Error> {
    if path == "-" {
        Ok(Box::new(BufReader::new(io::stdin())))
    } else {
        let f = File::open(path)?;
        Ok(Box::new(BufReader::new(f)))
    }
}

fn search_lines<R: BufRead>(reader: R, needle: &str) -> io::Result<()> {
    for line in reader.lines() {
        let line = line?;
        if line.contains(needle) {
            println!("{line}");
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

────────────────────────
10. Build & run
────────────────────────
$ cargo build --release
$ echo "hello world" | ./target/release/babygrep lo     # prints the line
$ ./target/release/babygrep foo src/main.rs             # search a file
$ ./target/release/babygrep fn src/*                    # multiple files

────────────────────────
11. Where ownership/borrowing showed up
────────────────────────
• pattern is owned (String) inside Config, but only shared (&str)
  during search, so we pass &cfg.pattern. Cheap: just a slice.

• open_buf returns Box<dyn BufRead>.  The Box owns the reader
  (either File or Stdin) and erases the exact concrete type, so the
  borrow checker does not need to know which one at compile time.

• search_lines<R: BufRead>(reader: R, …) takes ownership of `reader`
  by value; BufRead is not copy, so the stream is consumed exactly once.

────────────────────────
12. Niceties you can add next
────────────────────────
• Use the `clap` crate for richer CLI parsing (`cargo add clap`).  
• Colourize matches (`cargo add termcolor`).  
• Provide `-i` flag: needle.to_lowercase(), line.to_lowercase().  
• Stream, don’t allocate whole String for each line: use
  String::clear() & read_line().  
• Parallelise across files with Rayon.

────────────────────────
13. Take-away idioms
────────────────────────
• Bubble errors with `?` and return Result<T, Box<dyn Error>>  
• Own big things (String, Vec) at the edges, borrow inside hot loops  
• Trait objects (`Box<dyn BufRead>`) give dynamic dispatch when you
  want “any reader” without generics in outer code.  
• Keep main() tiny; write testable helpers.

That’s it—your first non-trivial Rust CLI!

With Jest you can have  

• one “baseline” mock that is loaded for every test via setupFilesAfterEnv  
• a completely different mock (or even the real implementation) that you
install only inside certain test files.

The trick is to remember two rules about Jest’s module system:

1. setupFiles / setupFilesAfterEnv are executed **before** each test file is
evaluated.  
2. `jest.mock()` calls that appear in a test file are **hoisted to the top of
that file** (before the first `import`/`require` in that file).

Because of (2) anything you mock in the test file will run **after** the code
in setupFilesAfterEnv and therefore can replace the “global” mock that was set
up there.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. Put a default mock in setupFilesAfterEnv
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// jest.setup.js   (add this file name to  jest.config.js → setupFilesAfterEnv)
jest.mock('my-api', () => ({
  fetchUser : jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({ id: 1, name: 'Default User' })),
  saveUser  : jest.fn(),
}));

// nothing is imported here – the mock will apply to every test file
module.exports = {};     // optional, keeps TypeScript happy

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2. Override that mock in a particular test file
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// user.admin.spec.ts
// ──────────────────
// This call is hoisted → it runs after jest.setup.js but before any import
jest.mock('my-api', () => ({
  fetchUser : jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({ id: 999, role: 'admin' })),
  saveUser  : jest.fn(),
}));

import { fetchUser } from 'my-api';   // gets the overridden version
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';

// …your tests
test('shows the admin name', async () => {
  expect(await fetchUser()).toEqual({ id: 999, role: 'admin' });
});

Nothing else is needed—the second `jest.mock` completely replaces the one that
ran in the setup file.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3. Overriding only in one describe / one test (dynamic mocking)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
If you want a **different** mock only inside a single `describe` or `it`, use
`jest.resetModules()` + `jest.doMock()`:

// user.dynamic.spec.ts
describe('special case', () => {
  beforeAll(() => {
    jest.resetModules();                 // clear the module registry

    jest.doMock('my-api', () => ({
      fetchUser : jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({ id: 42 })),
      saveUser  : jest.fn(),
    }));
  });

  it('returns user 42', async () => {
    const { fetchUser } = require('my-api');   // require after doMock
    expect(await fetchUser()).toEqual({ id: 42 });
  });
});

Outside that `describe` block the baseline mock from `jest.setup.js` is still
used.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4. Using the real implementation instead
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Sometimes you want the global mock but a single file should talk to the real
module:

// user.real.spec.ts
jest.unmock('my-api');               // remove the baseline mock
import * as realApi from 'my-api';   // this is the real implementation

// …tests that rely on the real code

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Summary
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

• Put your “every-test” mock in a file listed in `setupFilesAfterEnv`.  
• In any test file you can call `jest.mock('module', factory)` at the very top
  to override that mock for the whole file.  
• For per-test or per-describe overrides use `jest.resetModules()` +
  `jest.doMock()`.  
• Use `jest.unmock()` or `jest.requireActual()` if you need the real module in
  addition to (or instead of) the default mock.

