Below is a complete, self-contained `tailnet acl` file ( `ACLs → Edit ACLs` in the admin console) that fulfils the stated policy.

• There are three human groups:  
  – `group:nill` (full, unrestricted access)  
  – `group:family` (may ONLY use any node that advertises Exit-Node, nothing else)  
  – `group:servers` (users who own/operate the machines that wear the device-tag `tag:server`)  

• A device-tag `tag:server` is used for every machine that should be treated as a “server”.  
  (You can tag a machine with `--advertise-tags=server` or from the admin console.)

• Exit-nodes may be any machine that advertises `--advertise-exit-node`; such machines automatically get the special tag `tag:exit`.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

```json
{
  "version": 1,

  //
  // 1.  Define the three people-groups
  //
  "groups": {
    "group:nill":   [ "user:nill1@example.com",
                      "user:nill2@example.com" ],

    "group:family": [ "user:alice@example.com",
                      "user:bob@example.com"  ],

    "group:servers": [ "user:serveradmin@example.com" ]
  },

  //
  // 2.  Define who is allowed to apply the server tag
  //
  "tagOwners": {
    // Any member of group:servers (or anyone in group:nill) is allowed to
    // tag devices with `tag:server`.
    "tag:server": [ "group:servers",
                    "group:nill" ]
  },

  //
  // 3.  ACL rules
  //
  "acls": [

    //--------------------------------------------------------------------
    // a)  group:nill  →  everything, everywhere, all ports
    //--------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": [ "group:nill" ],
      "dst": [ "*:*" ]                 // all nodes, all ports, all protos
    },

    //--------------------------------------------------------------------
    // b)  group:servers  →  TCP 22, 80, 443 to every node
    //--------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": [ "group:servers", "tag:server" ],  // users OR the tagged boxes
      "proto": "tcp",
      "dst": [
        "*:22",
        "*:80",
        "*:443"
      ]
    },
    // (No other rule exists for group:servers, so every other port/protocol
    //  is implicitly denied.)

    //--------------------------------------------------------------------
    // c)  group:family  →  Allowed ONLY to use exit-nodes
    //--------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": [ "group:family" ],

      // An “exit-node permission” rule must mention:
      //   1) the exit-node itself          (tag:exit)
      //   2) the Internet address ranges   0.0.0.0/0 and ::/0
      "dst": [ "tag:exit", "0.0.0.0/0", "::/0" ]
    }
    // (No other rule for family, so direct access to any tailnet service
    //  is denied.)
  ],

  //
  // 4.  Optional test-cases – remove if you do not use the “ACL tests” box
  //
  "tests": [
    // nill group = full access
    { "src": "user:nill1@example.com",  "dst": "100.64.0.10:3389", "allow": true },

    // family group: cannot hit internal port 80…
    { "src": "user:alice@example.com",  "dst": "100.64.0.10:80",   "allow": false },
    // …but CAN go out to the Internet through any exit-node
    { "src": "user:alice@example.com",  "dst": "0.0.0.0:443",      "allow": true },

    // servers group: allowed on 22, 80, 443
    { "src": "user:serveradmin@example.com", "dst": "100.64.0.20:22",  "allow": true },
    { "src": "user:serveradmin@example.com", "dst": "100.64.0.20:8080","allow": false }
  ]
}
```

How it satisfies the requirements
---------------------------------
1. Anything in `group:nill` has a blanket rule (`*:*`) that matches first; they can reach every node on every port/protocol.
2. `group:family` has exactly one allow-rule that matches the combination “exit-node + 0.0.0.0/0 + ::/0”, which is the pattern Tailscale looks for when validating exit-node usage. No other `accept` rule applies to them, so they cannot talk to internal services.
3. `group:servers` (or any device tagged `tag:server`) is limited to TCP ports 22, 80, 443 by the single dedicated rule; the lack of additional rules means all other ports and protocols are denied by the default-deny posture.

Simply adjust the e-mail addresses and (optionally) remove the test-cases section before saving the ACL.

Below is a compact but reasonably complete TypeScript “TCP layer” that sits on top of an IPv4 packet that you already have in a Uint8Array.  

It does **not** try to implement the full, state-machine driven TCP stack (that would run to hundreds of lines and still be incomplete); instead it gives you:

• IPv4 header parser (enough fields to be useful)  
• TCP header parser (all standard fields + options)  
• Correct RFC 793 checksum calculation, including the IPv4 pseudo-header  
• Convenience wrappers so you can do

```ts
const tcp = TCPv4.parse(ipPacketUint8Array);
console.log(tcp.header.flags.syn, tcp.payload.length);
```

You can extend the returned objects to build a full TCP socket/state machine if you wish.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tcp.ts
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
```ts
/*  Simple IPv4 + TCP parser  (c) 2024  –  public domain or MIT / your choice */

export interface IPv4Header {
    version:            4;
    ihl:                number;   // bytes
    dscp:               number;
    totalLength:        number;
    identification:     number;
    flags:              { df: boolean; mf: boolean; };
    fragmentOffset:     number;
    ttl:                number;
    protocol:           number;   // should be 6 for TCP
    headerChecksum:     number;
    src:                string;   // “1.2.3.4”
    dst:                string;
    options:            Uint8Array; // 0-byte if none
}

export interface TCPHeader {
    srcPort:        number;
    dstPort:        number;
    seq:            number;
    ack:            number;
    dataOffset:     number;           // bytes
    flags:          {
        ns: boolean; cwr: boolean; ece: boolean; urg: boolean;
        ack: boolean; psh: boolean; rst: boolean; syn: boolean; fin: boolean;
    };
    windowSize:     number;
    checksum:       number;
    urgentPointer:  number;
    options:        Uint8Array;       // 0-byte if none
}

export interface TCPDatagram {
    ipv4:      IPv4Header;
    header:    TCPHeader;
    payload:   Uint8Array;
    checksumOK:boolean;
}

/* ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

function readUint16(b: Uint8Array, off: number): number {
    return (b[off] << 8) | b[off + 1];
}
function readUint32(b: Uint8Array, off: number): number {
    return  (b[off]     << 24) |
            (b[off + 1] << 16) |
            (b[off + 2] <<  8) |
            (b[off + 3]      );
}

/* ----------  IPv4  ------------------------------------------------------ */
export function parseIPv4(pkt: Uint8Array): IPv4Header {
    if (pkt.length < 20) throw new Error("Truncated IPv4 header");

    const verIhl = pkt[0];
    const version = verIhl >> 4;
    if (version !== 4) throw new Error("Not IPv4");

    const ihlWords = verIhl & 0x0f;
    const ihlBytes = ihlWords * 4;
    if (pkt.length < ihlBytes) throw new Error("IPv4 header shorter than IHL");

    const dscp      = pkt[1];
    const totalLen  = readUint16(pkt, 2);
    const ident     = readUint16(pkt, 4);
    const flagsFO   = readUint16(pkt, 6);
    const flags = { df: !!(flagsFO & 0x4000),
                    mf: !!(flagsFO & 0x2000) };
    const fragmentOffset = flagsFO & 0x1fff;

    const ttl       = pkt[8];
    const proto     = pkt[9];
    const hdrCsum   = readUint16(pkt,10);

    const src = `${pkt[12]}.${pkt[13]}.${pkt[14]}.${pkt[15]}`;
    const dst = `${pkt[16]}.${pkt[17]}.${pkt[18]}.${pkt[19]}`;

    const optionsLen = ihlBytes - 20;
    const options    = optionsLen ? pkt.slice(20, 20 + optionsLen) : new Uint8Array(0);

    return {
        version: 4, ihl: ihlBytes, dscp, totalLength: totalLen,
        identification: ident, flags, fragmentOffset, ttl,
        protocol: proto, headerChecksum: hdrCsum, src, dst, options
    };
}

/* ----------  TCP  ------------------------------------------------------- */

export function parseTCP(pkt: Uint8Array, off=0): {header:TCPHeader, payload:Uint8Array} {
    if (pkt.length - off < 20) throw new Error("Truncated TCP header");

    const srcPort   = readUint16(pkt, off + 0);
    const dstPort   = readUint16(pkt, off + 2);
    const seq       = readUint32(pkt, off + 4) >>> 0;
    const ack       = readUint32(pkt, off + 8) >>> 0;

    const dataOffsetAndFlags = readUint16(pkt, off + 12);

    const dataOffsetWords = dataOffsetAndFlags >> 12;
    const dataOffsetBytes = dataOffsetWords * 4;
    if (pkt.length - off < dataOffsetBytes)
        throw new Error("TCP header shorter than data offset");

    const flagsField = dataOffsetAndFlags & 0x0fff;
    const flags = {
        ns : !!(flagsField & 0x100),
        cwr: !!(flagsField & 0x080),
        ece: !!(flagsField & 0x040),
        urg: !!(flagsField & 0x020),
        ack: !!(flagsField & 0x010),
        psh: !!(flagsField & 0x008),
        rst: !!(flagsField & 0x004),
        syn: !!(flagsField & 0x002),
        fin: !!(flagsField & 0x001),
    };

    const window     = readUint16(pkt, off + 14);
    const checksum   = readUint16(pkt, off + 16);
    const urgentPtr  = readUint16(pkt, off + 18);

    const optionsLen = dataOffsetBytes - 20;
    const options    = optionsLen ? pkt.slice(off + 20, off + 20 + optionsLen)
                                  : new Uint8Array(0);

    const payload = pkt.subarray(off + dataOffsetBytes);

    return {
        header: {
            srcPort, dstPort, seq, ack,
            dataOffset: dataOffsetBytes,
            flags, windowSize: window, checksum,
            urgentPointer: urgentPtr,
            options
        },
        payload
    };
}

/* ----------  Check-sum -------------------------------------------------- */

function onesComplementAdd16(a: number, b: number): number {
    const sum = (a + b) & 0xFFFF;
    return sum + (sum >>> 16);        // fold carry
}

/* 16-bit one’s complement of all 16-bit words in buf (big-endian) */
function checksum16(buf: Uint8Array): number {
    let sum = 0;
    let i   = 0;

    while (i + 1 < buf.length) {
        sum = onesComplementAdd16(sum, (buf[i] << 8) | buf[i + 1]);
        i += 2;
    }
    if (i < buf.length) {             // odd byte
        sum = onesComplementAdd16(sum, buf[i] << 8);
    }
    return (~sum) & 0xFFFF;
}

/* Full TCP checksum – needs IPv4 pseudo-header */
export function tcpChecksum(ip: IPv4Header,
                            tcpSeg: Uint8Array): number {

    const pseudo = new Uint8Array(12 + tcpSeg.length);
    const p = pseudo;

    const ipParts = ip.src.split('.').map(Number)
               .concat(ip.dst.split('.').map(Number));
    for (let i = 0; i < 8; ++i) p[i] = ipParts[i];

    p[8]  = 0;               // zero
    p[9]  = 6;               // protocol = TCP
    p[10] = (tcpSeg.length >> 8) & 0xff;
    p[11] = (tcpSeg.length) & 0xff;

    p.set(tcpSeg, 12);

    return checksum16(pseudo);
}

/* ----------  One-shot convenience  -------------------------------------- */

export const TCPv4 = {
    parse(pkt: Uint8Array): TCPDatagram {
        const ipv4 = parseIPv4(pkt);
        if (ipv4.protocol !== 6)
            throw new Error("IPv4 protocol ≠ TCP (6)");

        const {header, payload} = parseTCP(pkt, ipv4.ihl);
        const tcpSlice = pkt.subarray(ipv4.ihl);   // header+payload, as in wire
        const good = tcpChecksum(ipv4, tcpSlice) === 0;

        return { ipv4, header, payload, checksumOK: good };
    }
};
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example usage
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
```ts
import { TCPv4 } from "./tcp";

// rawIPv4 is a Uint8Array you already possess (Ethernet/IP sniff, etc.)
function demo(rawIPv4: Uint8Array) {
    const seg = TCPv4.parse(rawIPv4);

    console.log("from", seg.ipv4.src + ":" + seg.header.srcPort,
                "to",   seg.ipv4.dst + ":" + seg.header.dstPort);

    console.log("SEQ=", seg.header.seq,
                "ACK=", seg.header.ack,
                "SYN=", seg.header.flags.syn,
                "ACK flag=", seg.header.flags.ack,
                "payload bytes=", seg.payload.length,
                "checksum OK?", seg.checksumOK);
}
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is covered / where to go next
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
• Parser is RFC-791 / RFC-793 compliant for the header pieces that matter.  
• TCP options are returned raw; you can add a helper to parse e.g. MSS, timestamps, SACK permitted.  
• If you want a full TCP stack (state machine, retransmission, congestion, etc.) you can now build it on top of the parsed information:

 1. Keep a map keyed by 4-tuple (src/dst IP, src/dst port).  
 2. Store SEQ/ACK state, window, timers.  
 3. Handle control flags (SYN/FIN/RST).  
 4. Use tcpChecksum when crafting outgoing packets.  

That is beyond a “single-file answer”, but the heavy lifting—correct header decoding and checksum—has been done here.

Yes, you can get the “`Util.doSomething` / `Util.doSomethingElse`” style in two different ways.  
Which one you pick just depends on where you want the grouping to happen—inside the
importing file or inside the module that exports the functions.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. Group on the *import* side (recommended, nothing extra
   to write in the module itself)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

util.js
```js
// Any number of named exports
export function doSomething()  { … }
export function doSomethingElse()  { … }
// …more functions
```

some-other-file.js
```js
// “namespace import” gathers *all* named exports into the object “Util”
import * as Util from './util.js';

Util.doSomething();
Util.doSomethingElse();
```

You don’t need a default export at all; the `import * as …` syntax automatically
builds the object for you.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2. Group inside the module itself (export an object or
   class as the default)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

util.js
```js
function doSomething()  { … }
function doSomethingElse()  { … }

export { doSomething, doSomethingElse };      // still allow named imports
export default {                              // default value is an object
  doSomething,
  doSomethingElse
};
```

some-other-file.js
```js
import Util from './util.js';

Util.doSomething();
Util.doSomethingElse();
```

You can also make the default export a class with static methods:

```js
export default class Util {
  static doSomething()      { … }
  static doSomethingElse()  { … }
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Key points
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
• ES-modules do not create that “`Util` object” automatically for you; you either
  use a namespace import (`import * as Util …`) or explicitly export an
  aggregate object/class as the default.  
• A module can have many named exports but only one default export.  
• You can mix the two styles: other modules can choose `import * as Util …`,
  `import { doSomething } …`, or `import Util …` depending on what you exported.

