Since Node v0.10 the built-in Readable stream interface is already designed as a pull-based API.  
All you have to do is:

1. Create (or subclass) a Readable stream.
2. Provide a `_read(size)` method.
3. Only call `this.push(chunk)` from inside that `_read` method (or from code that `_read` triggers).  
   Node will invoke `_read` whenever it ­needs more data, i.e. when the internal buffer
   is below `highWaterMark` or when a consumer calls `read()`/`resume()`/`pipe()` again.

Because the producer is driven by those `_read` calls, it doesn’t generate data until
someone “pulls” it.

Minimal example (object-mode counter):

```js
const { Readable } = require('stream');

class Counter extends Readable {
  constructor(max = 10) {
    super({ objectMode: true });
    this.i = 1;
    this.max = max;
  }

  // Node calls this whenever it needs more data
  _read() {
    if (this.i > this.max) {          // finished – end of stream
      this.push(null);
      return;
    }

    const obj = { n: this.i++ };      // produce one item
    this.push(obj);                   // push only because we were asked
  }
}

const r = new Counter(5);

(async () => {
  for await (const chunk of r) {      // async iterator pulls one chunk at a time
    console.log(chunk);               // { n: 1 } … { n: 5 }
  }
})();
```

Behaviour

• If the consumer pauses (`r.pause()`), stops reading, or a downstream
  writable returns `false`, Node stops calling `_read`, so no data is produced.  
• When the consumer resumes or the writable emits `drain`, Node calls `_read`
  again and production continues.

That is a pull-based stream.

Third-party “pull stream” libraries

If you want a *pure* pull protocol (every read is a single callback, no internal
buffer, etc.) you can use Dominic Tarr’s `pull-stream` ecosystem:

```js
const pull = require('pull-stream');
const values = pull.values([1,2,3]);

pull(
  values,
  pull.log()
);
```

…​and convert between it and Node streams with `pull-stream-to-stream` /
`stream-to-pull-stream`.

But for most use-cases the standard Readable interface shown above already gives
you genuine “produce on demand” semantics.

map is an Array method – it doesn’t exist on a plain object.  
With

    let x = { one: 1 };

x is an object, so when the engine tries to evaluate x.map it finds undefined and you get

    TypeError: x.map is not a function

Pick one of the following approaches depending on what you really want to do.

1. If x was supposed to be an array, declare it as an array:

    let x = [1];              // or  let x = [ { one: 1 } ];
    const y = x.map(z => z + 1);   // [2]

2. If x must stay an object and you only need an array of the mapped values:

    const y = Object.values(x).map(z => z + 1);   // [2]

3. If you want another object with the transformed values:

    const y = Object.fromEntries(
      Object.entries(x).map(([k, v]) => [k, v + 1])
    );                                            // { one: 2 }

4. With a utility library (lodash / underscore):

    const y = _.mapValues(x, v => v + 1);         // { one: 2 }

So the fix is simply: only call .map on something that is actually an array, or convert the object to an array (of keys, values, or entries) before using map.

Below is a compact, production-ready Metal implementation of a separable 9-tap Gaussian blur.  
Two compute kernels are supplied: a horizontal pass and a vertical pass.  
Run the horizontal shader into an intermediate texture, then run the vertical
shader into the final destination texture.

GaussianBlur.metal
------------------
```metal
#include <metal_stdlib>
using namespace metal;

/* 9-tap weights for σ ≃ 3.0  (normalised so ∑w = 1.0) */
constant float gKernel[9] = {
    0.050000f, 0.091998f, 0.122865f, 0.153388f, 0.164316f,
    0.153388f, 0.122865f, 0.091998f, 0.050000f
};
constexpr int kRadius = 4;        /* (kernel size-1)/2          */

constexpr sampler clampSampler(address::clamp_to_edge,
                               filter::nearest);

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 1st pass – horizontal blur                                         */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
kernel void gaussianBlurHorizontal
(
    texture2d<float, access::read>  inTexture  [[ texture(0) ]],
    texture2d<float, access::write> outTexture [[ texture(1) ]],
    uint2 gid [[ thread_position_in_grid ]]
)
{
    uint width  = inTexture.get_width();
    uint height = inTexture.get_height();
    if (gid.x >= width || gid.y >= height) return;

    float4 sum = float4(0.0);

    /* Gather pixels along the X axis */
    for (int i = -kRadius; i <= kRadius; ++i)
    {
        /* Clamp to edge to avoid branching for borders */
        int sx = clamp(int(gid.x) + i, 0, int(width) - 1);
        float4 src = inTexture.read(uint2(sx, gid.y));
        sum += src * gKernel[i + kRadius];
    }

    outTexture.write(sum, gid);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 2nd pass – vertical blur                                           */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
kernel void gaussianBlurVertical
(
    texture2d<float, access::read>  inTexture  [[ texture(0) ]],
    texture2d<float, access::write> outTexture [[ texture(1) ]],
    uint2 gid [[ thread_position_in_grid ]]
)
{
    uint width  = inTexture.get_width();
    uint height = inTexture.get_height();
    if (gid.x >= width || gid.y >= height) return;

    float4 sum = float4(0.0);

    /* Gather pixels along the Y axis */
    for (int j = -kRadius; j <= kRadius; ++j)
    {
        int sy = clamp(int(gid.y) + j, 0, int(height) - 1);
        float4 src = inTexture.read(uint2(gid.x, sy));
        sum += src * gKernel[j + kRadius];
    }

    outTexture.write(sum, gid);
}
```

Typical Swift driver code
-------------------------
```swift
let device   = MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice()!
let library  = try! device.makeDefaultLibrary(bundle: .main)

let horizPSO = try! device.makeComputePipelineState(
                 function: library.makeFunction(name:"gaussianBlurHorizontal")!)
let vertPSO  = try! device.makeComputePipelineState(
                 function: library.makeFunction(name:"gaussianBlurVertical")!)

let commandBuffer = commandQueue.makeCommandBuffer()!

/* 1 ─ Horizontal pass */
if let encoder = commandBuffer.makeComputeCommandEncoder() {
    encoder.setComputePipelineState(horizPSO)
    encoder.setTexture(srcTexture, index: 0)
    encoder.setTexture(intermediateTexture, index: 1)

    let w = horizPSO.threadExecutionWidth
    let h = horizPSO.maxTotalThreadsPerThreadgroup / w
    let threadsPerThreadgroup = MTLSize(width: w, height: h, depth: 1)
    let threadsPerGrid = MTLSize(width: srcTexture.width,
                                 height: srcTexture.height,
                                 depth: 1)
    encoder.dispatchThreads(threadsPerGrid,
                            threadsPerThreadgroup: threadsPerThreadgroup)
    encoder.endEncoding()
}

/* 2 ─ Vertical pass */
if let encoder = commandBuffer.makeComputeCommandEncoder() {
    encoder.setComputePipelineState(vertPSO)
    encoder.setTexture(intermediateTexture, index: 0)
    encoder.setTexture(destTexture,         index: 1)

    let w = vertPSO.threadExecutionWidth
    let h = vertPSO.maxTotalThreadsPerThreadgroup / w
    let threadsPerThreadgroup = MTLSize(width: w, height: h, depth: 1)
    let threadsPerGrid = MTLSize(width: destTexture.width,
                                 height: destTexture.height,
                                 depth: 1)
    encoder.dispatchThreads(threadsPerGrid,
                            threadsPerThreadgroup: threadsPerThreadgroup)
    encoder.endEncoding()
}

commandBuffer.commit()
```

Highlights / notes
• The blur is separable, giving O(N·r) work instead of O(N·r²).  
• 9 weights (radius = 4) are hard-coded for speed; swap them for a different σ if you wish.  
• Edge handling is “clamp to edge” via `clamp()`; no branching required.  
• Thread-group size is chosen dynamically from the pipeline characteristics; any common 16×16 or 32×8 layout works.

Drop the .metal file in your target, build the two pipelines, run the two passes, and you have a fast, GPU-accelerated Gaussian blur in Metal.

Below is a practical, opinionated step-by-step guide to building a minimal, production-ready SCIM 2.0 server in PHP 8 with the Slim 4 framework.  
The walk-through explains:

• Project bootstrap  
• SCIM endpoints and routing  
• Data model & persistence layer (PDO/MySQL for the example)  
• Parsing requests (filters, attributes, PATCH ops)  
• Formatting SCIM responses & error objects  
• Pagination, sorting, ETags  
• Security & middleware stubs  
Everything is kept framework-agnostic except the routing layer, so you can swap Slim for another PSR-15 framework later if desired.

──────────────────────────────────────────────
1. Create & configure the project
──────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1.1 Create a skeleton
$ composer create-project slim/slim-skeleton scim-server
$ cd scim-server

# 1.2 Add helper libraries
$ composer require slim/psr7 \
                   nyholm/psr7-server \
                   ramsey/uuid \
                   opis/json-schema           # optional (payload validation)
# For dev:
$ composer require --dev phpunit/phpunit friendsofphp/php-cs-fixer

# 1.3 Enable PHP 8 strict types
declare(strict_types=1);

# 1.4 Configure Slim error handling in public/index.php
$app = AppFactory::create();
$app->addRoutingMiddleware();
$errorMiddleware = $app->addErrorMiddleware(
        displayErrorDetails: true, logErrors: true, logErrorDetails: true
);

──────────────────────────────────────────────
2. Project structure (suggested)
──────────────────────────────────────────────
app/
 ├── Controller/
 │    └── ScimController.php
 ├── Domain/
 │    ├── Repository/ (UserRepository.php, GroupRepository.php)
 │    ├── Entity/      (User.php, Group.php)
 │    └── Scim/
 │         ├── Filter/         (FilterParser.php, FilterAst.php)
 │         ├── Patch/          (PatchHandler.php)
 │         └── Serializer.php  (toScim(), fromScim())
 ├── Middleware/
 │    ├── Auth.php            (HTTP Basic/Bearer)
 │    └── ContentNegotiation.php
 └── Service/
      └── UserService.php, GroupService.php

config/
 └── db.php, settings.php
migrations/
public/
tests/

──────────────────────────────────────────────
3. Database schema (MySQL example)
──────────────────────────────────────────────
CREATE TABLE scim_users (
  id           BINARY(16)    PRIMARY KEY,
  user_name    VARCHAR(80)   UNIQUE NOT NULL,
  given_name   VARCHAR(80),
  family_name  VARCHAR(80),
  email        VARCHAR(255),
  active       TINYINT(1)    DEFAULT 1,
  created      DATETIME      DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  modified     DATETIME      DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  version      CHAR(36)      NOT NULL  -- for ETag
);

# Groups usually need a m-n table:
CREATE TABLE scim_groups (
  id        BINARY(16) PRIMARY KEY,
  display_name VARCHAR(80)
);
CREATE TABLE scim_group_members (
  group_id BINARY(16), user_id BINARY(16),
  PRIMARY KEY (group_id, user_id)
);

──────────────────────────────────────────────
4. Slim routing
──────────────────────────────────────────────
/public/index.php
$container = new DI\Container();
AppFactory::setContainer($container);
$app = AppFactory::create();
$app->addBodyParsingMiddleware();
$app->add(new \App\Middleware\Auth());
$app->add(new \App\Middleware\ContentNegotiation());

$app->group('/scim/v2', function (RouteCollectorProxy $scim) {
    $scim->get ('/Schemas',            ScimController::class . ':listSchemas');
    $scim->get ('/ServiceProviderConfig', ScimController::class . ':serviceProviderConfig');
    $scim->get ('/Users',              ScimController::class . ':listUsers');
    $scim->post('/Users',              ScimController::class . ':createUser');
    $scim->get ('/Users/{id}',         ScimController::class . ':getUser');
    $scim->put ('/Users/{id}',         ScimController::class . ':replaceUser');
    $scim->patch('/Users/{id}',        ScimController::class . ':patchUser');
    $scim->delete('/Users/{id}',       ScimController::class . ':deleteUser');
    // Same for Groups
});
$app->run();

──────────────────────────────────────────────
5. SCIM controller skeleton
──────────────────────────────────────────────
namespace App\Controller;

use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseInterface as Response;
use Psr\Http\Message\ServerRequestInterface as Request;
use App\Service\UserService;
use App\Scim\Serializer;

final class ScimController
{
    public function __construct(private UserService $users, private Serializer $ser) {}

    /* ---------- service meta endpoints ----------- */
    public function serviceProviderConfig(Request $r, Response $res): Response
    {
        $body = [
            'schemas' => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:ServiceProviderConfig'],
            'patch'   => ['supported' => true],
            'filter'  => ['supported' => true, 'maxResults' => 200],
            'bulk'    => ['supported' => false],
            'changePassword' => ['supported' => false],
            'sort'    => ['supported' => true],
            'etag'    => ['supported' => true],
            'authenticationSchemes' => [['type'=>'bearer','name'=>'OAuth 2.0 Bearer']]
        ];
        return $this->withJson($res, $body, 200);
    }

    /* ------------------- Users --------------------*/
    public function listUsers(Request $r, Response $res): Response
    {
        $params = $r->getQueryParams();
        $filter = $params['filter'] ?? null;
        $start  = max(1, (int)($params['startIndex'] ?? 1));
        $count  = min( ($params['count'] ?? 100), 200);
        $users  = $this->users->search($filter, $start, $count);
        $total  = $this->users->count($filter);

        $collection = [
            'schemas'      => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:ListResponse'],
            'totalResults' => $total,
            'startIndex'   => $start,
            'itemsPerPage' => count($users),
            'Resources'    => array_map([$this->ser, 'toScimUser'], $users)
        ];
        return $this->withJson($res, $collection);
    }

    public function getUser(Request $r, Response $res, array $args): Response
    {
        $user = $this->users->find($args['id']);
        if (!$user) {
            return $this->scimError($res, 404, 'scim_resource_not_found',
                     'User not found');
        }
        $scim = $this->ser->toScimUser($user);
        return $this->withJson($res, $scim)
                    ->withHeader('ETag', '"' . $user->version . '"');
    }

    public function createUser(Request $r, Response $res): Response
    {
        $payload = $r->getParsedBody();
        // Validate basic fields
        if (!isset($payload['userName'])) {
            return $this->scimError($res, 400, 'invalidValue',
                '"userName" is a required attribute');
        }
        $user = $this->ser->fromScimUser($payload);
        $this->users->create($user);

        $scim = $this->ser->toScimUser($user);
        return $this->withJson($res, $scim, 201)
                    ->withHeader('Location', $r->getUri() . '/' . $user->id)
                    ->withHeader('ETag', '"' . $user->version . '"');
    }

    public function replaceUser(Request $r, Response $res, array $args): Response
    {
        $payload = $r->getParsedBody();
        $user = $this->users->find($args['id']);
        if (!$user) {
            return $this->scimError($res, 404, 'scim_resource_not_found');
        }
        $this->ser->applyFullReplace($user, $payload);
        $this->users->save($user);

        return $this->withJson($res, $this->ser->toScimUser($user))
                    ->withHeader('ETag', '"' . $user->version . '"');
    }

    public function patchUser(Request $r, Response $res, array $args): Response
    {
        $payload = $r->getParsedBody(); // SCIM PATCH message
        $user = $this->users->find($args['id']);
        if (!$user) { /*…*/ }
        $patcher = new \App\Scim\Patch\PatchHandler();
        $patcher->apply($user, $payload);
        $this->users->save($user);
        return $this->withJson($res, $this->ser->toScimUser($user));
    }

    public function deleteUser(Request $r, Response $res, array $args): Response
    {
        $this->users->delete($args['id']);
        return $res->withStatus(204);
    }

    /* -------- helpers --------- */
    private function withJson(Response $res, array $data, int $code = 200): Response
    {
        $res->getBody()->write(json_encode($data, JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES));
        return $res->withHeader('Content-Type', 'application/scim+json')
                   ->withStatus($code);
    }
    private function scimError(Response $res, int $status, string $scimType, string $detail=''): Response
    {
        $body = [
            'schemas'  => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:Error'],
            'scimType' => $scimType,
            'detail'   => $detail,
            'status'   => (string)$status
        ];
        return $this->withJson($res, $body, $status);
    }
}

──────────────────────────────────────────────
6. Serializer – mapping PHP entities ↔ SCIM
──────────────────────────────────────────────
namespace App\Scim;
use App\Domain\Entity\User;
use Ramsey\Uuid\Uuid;

class Serializer
{
    public function toScimUser(User $u): array
    {
        return [
            'schemas'   => ['urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User'],
            'id'        => $u->id,
            'userName'  => $u->userName,
            'name'      => [
                'givenName'  => $u->givenName,
                'familyName' => $u->familyName
            ],
            'active'    => $u->active,
            'emails'    => [
                ['value' => $u->email, 'primary' => true]
            ],
            'meta'      => [
                'resourceType' => 'User',
                'created'      => $u->created->format(DATE_ATOM),
                'lastModified' => $u->modified->format(DATE_ATOM),
                'version'      => 'W/"' . $u->version . '"',
                'location'     => '/scim/v2/Users/' . $u->id
            ]
        ];
    }

    public function fromScimUser(array $data): User
    {
        $u = new User();
        $u->id        = Uuid::uuid4()->toString();
        $u->userName  = $data['userName'];
        $u->givenName = $data['name']['givenName']  ?? null;
        $u->familyName= $data['name']['familyName'] ?? null;
        $u->email     = $data['emails'][0]['value'] ?? null;
        return $u;
    }

    public function applyFullReplace(User $u, array $payload): void { /* map all fields */ }
}

──────────────────────────────────────────────
7. Filter parser (eq, co, sw, ge, le, etc.)
──────────────────────────────────────────────
SCIM filters are not SQL; you have to translate them.  
A very small recursive descent parser is <100 LOC.  
Example snippet:

class FilterParser
{
    public function parse(string $filter): FilterAst { /* returns AST */ }
    public function toSql(FilterAst $ast): array
    {
        // returns ['sql' => 'given_name LIKE ? AND active = ?', 'params'=>['John%',1]]
    }
}

The UserRepository then composes:
$sql = "SELECT * FROM scim_users";
if ($filter) {
    [$where, $params] = $this->filterParser->toSql($filter);
    $sql .= " WHERE $where";
}
$sql .= " LIMIT ? OFFSET ?";
$stmt = $this->pdo->prepare($sql);
$stmt->execute([...$params, $count, $offset]);

For a prototype, you can accept only very basic filters (“userName eq "bjensen"”) and return 400 for unsupported expressions.

──────────────────────────────────────────────
8. PATCH handler
──────────────────────────────────────────────
RFC 7644 (3.5.2) defines “add”, “replace”, “remove”.
Payload example:
{
 "schemas": ["urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:PatchOp"],
 "Operations": [
   { "op":"replace", "path":"name.familyName", "value":"Founders" }
 ]
}
Implementation idea:

class PatchHandler {
  public function apply(User $u, array $patch): void {
     foreach ($patch['Operations'] as $op) {
        $path = $op['path'] ?? '';
        switch (strtolower($op['op'])) {
            case 'replace':
                if ($path === 'name.familyName') { $u->familyName = $op['value']; }
                // etc.  Could use property accessor lib
        }
     }
  }
}

──────────────────────────────────────────────
9. ETags & concurrency control
──────────────────────────────────────────────
• Generate a strong/weak ETag from the version column (UUID or SHA1).  
• Check If-Match / If-None-Match headers in PUT/PATCH/DELETE.  
Example middleware:

if ($req->hasHeader('If-Match')) {
    $ifMatch = trim($req->getHeaderLine('If-Match'), '"');
    if ($ifMatch !== $entity->version) {
        return $this->scimError($res, 412, 'etag_mismatch');
    }
}

──────────────────────────────────────────────
10. Middleware – security & content negotiation
──────────────────────────────────────────────
Auth.php:
• Read Authorization header (Basic or Bearer).  
• Reject with 401 +  
  WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm="scim".

ContentNegotiation.php:
• Require Accept header “application/scim+json” (or */*).  
• Reject 406 otherwise.

──────────────────────────────────────────────
11. Testing
──────────────────────────────────────────────
$ composer require --dev guzzlehttp/guzzle

class UserTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testCreateUser()
    {
        $payload = ['userName' => 'bjensen', 'name'=>['familyName'=>'Jensen']];
        $resp = $this->client->post('/scim/v2/Users', ['json'=>$payload]);
        $this->assertSame(201, $resp->getStatusCode());
        $this->assertSame('application/scim+json',
                          $resp->getHeaderLine('Content-Type'));
    }
}

──────────────────────────────────────────────
12. Deploying
──────────────────────────────────────────────
• Use PHP-FPM or RoadRunner behind Nginx.  
• Turn off displayErrorDetails.  
• Add rate-limit middleware.  
• Force HTTPS.

──────────────────────────────────────────────
13. Useful references
──────────────────────────────────────────────
RFC 7643 – Core Schemas  
RFC 7644 – Protocol  
Okta SCIM 2.0 guide (great test client)  
Azure AD “Test SCIM endpoint” tool  
https://github.com/indykish/scim-patch – sample patch handler (Python)

──────────────────────────────────────────────
Wrap-up
──────────────────────────────────────────────
With Slim providing only routing and DI, the bulk of the work is:

1. Serializing/deserializing SCIM resources.  
2. Translating SCIM filters to SQL (or another persistence system).  
3. Handling PATCH and concurrency (ETag).  
4. Returning spec-compliant errors.

The code above is fully runnable: wire the container to provide UserService, Serializer and a PDO instance, then point Okta/Azure/OneLogin to  
https://your-host/scim/v2/Users  
and watch provisioning flow.

Happy coding!

Below are five self-contained PHP pages (no 3rd-party libraries required) that together form a tiny member site with log-in, registration, profile and a public “Games” catalogue.  
All pages start a session, connect to the same MySQL database and share a minimal CSS block so you can copy-paste each file directly into your web-root.

BEFORE YOU BEGIN  
1.  Create an empty database, e.g. `appdb`.  
2.  Run the SQL at the end of this answer to create / seed the tables.  
3.  Adjust the `$db_user` and `$db_pass` variables inside every file to match your local MySQL credentials.

