“Coroutines” are not part of core PHP – you need a runtime (Swoole, AMPHP, ReactPHP, etc.) that actually schedules them.  
The way you limit the amount of work that can run in parallel is always the same, no matter which runtime you choose: use a semaphore / channel (sometimes called a pool) and let every coroutine take a ‟token’’ before it starts and give it back when it finishes.

Below are three typical ways to do it with the tools that are most often used together with PHP 8.2.  
Pick the one that fits the library or extension you already run.  
(All examples launch 30 jobs but never let more than 5 run at the same time.)

────────────────────────────────────
1. Swoole (ext-swoole)

```php
<?php
// php -dextension=swoole yourfile.php
Co\run(function () {
    $max     = 5;
    $sem     = new Swoole\Coroutine\Semaphore($max); // the 5 tokens
    $wg      = new Swoole\Coroutine\WaitGroup();     // to wait for everything

    foreach (range(1, 30) as $n) {
        $wg->add();
        go(function () use ($sem, $wg, $n) {
            $sem->wait();               // ↓ blocks if 5 are already working
            defer(function () use ($sem, $wg) {
                $sem->notify();          // give the token back
                $wg->done();             // mark coroutine done
            });

            echo "start $n\n";
            Co::sleep(1);                // pretend to do something
            echo "end   $n\n";
        });
    }

    $wg->wait();                         // wait for all 30 jobs
});
```

You can do the same with a `Swoole\Coroutine\Channel` (capacity 5) if you like channels more than semaphores.

────────────────────────────────────
2. AMPHP (Fibers / EventLoop based, composer package ‟amphp”)

```php
<?php
require 'vendor/autoload.php';

use Amp\Sync\LocalSemaphore;
use function Amp\async;
use function Amp\await;

$sem      = new LocalSemaphore(5);   // 5 parallel slots
$promises = [];

foreach (range(1, 30) as $i) {
    $promises[] = async(function () use ($sem, $i) {
        $lock = $sem->acquire();      // waits until a slot is free
        try {
            echo "start $i\n";
            // any async code here, e.g. `Amp\delay(1);`
            sleep(1);                 // just demo
            echo "end   $i\n";
        } finally {
            $lock->release();         // free the slot
        }
    });
}

await(Amp\Promise\all($promises));    // block until everything is finished
```

────────────────────────────────────
3. “Pure” PHP 8.2 using Fibers only  
(Useful if you do not want another library; you still have to drive the event loop yourself.)

```php
<?php
class Pool {
    private int $limit;
    private SplQueue $queue;
    private SplObjectStorage $running;

    public function __construct(int $limit = 5) {
        $this->limit   = $limit;
        $this->queue   = new SplQueue;
        $this->running = new SplObjectStorage;
    }

    public function add(Closure $job): void {
        $this->queue->enqueue($job);
        $this->tick();
    }

    public function run(): void {
        while (!$this->queue->isEmpty() || $this->running->count()) {
            foreach ($this->running as $fiber) {
                if ($fiber->isTerminated()) {
                    $this->running->detach($fiber);
                }
            }
            $this->tick();
            usleep(1000);             // crude “event loop”
        }
    }

    private function tick(): void {
        while ($this->running->count() < $this->limit && !$this->queue->isEmpty()) {
            $fn    = $this->queue->dequeue();
            $fiber = new Fiber(function () use ($fn) {
                $fn();
            });
            $this->running->attach($fiber);
            $fiber->start();
        }
    }
}

// demo
$pool = new Pool(5);
foreach (range(1, 30) as $n) {
    $pool->add(function () use ($n) {
        echo "start $n\n";
        sleep(1);
        echo "end   $n\n";
    });
}
$pool->run();
```

Of course, once you need network I/O or timers you’ll usually switch to an established async library (AMP, ReactPHP, Swoole) instead of writing your own loop.

────────────────────────────────────
Key take-away

1. Create a semaphore / channel with the desired capacity (5).  
2. Every coroutine takes a token before it starts; if no token is available it waits.  
3. After the job finishes, the coroutine releases the token so another job can start.

That pattern works in Swoole, AMPHP, ReactPHP, or any other coroutine/async environment you use with PHP 8.2.

The vulnerability exists because the application builds the XPath query by simple string concatenation, so whatever the attacker puts in user or pass becomes part of the XPath syntax.  
Instead of concatenating, compile a fixed XPath that contains placeholders and let the XPath engine supply the user-controlled values.  The standard JAXP XPath API lets you do this with variables:

```java
import javax.xml.xpath.*;
import javax.xml.namespace.QName;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;

public class SafeLogin {

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Return the <user> node that matches name & pass, or null if not found
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    public static org.w3c.dom.Node getUserNode(Document doc,
                                               final String user,
                                               final String pass)
                                                throws XPathExpressionException {

        XPathFactory factory = XPathFactory.newInstance();
        XPath        xpath   = factory.newXPath();

        /* 1. Bind run-time values to variable names $uname and $pwd           */
        xpath.setXPathVariableResolver(new XPathVariableResolver() {
            @Override
            public Object resolveVariable(QName varName) {
                switch (varName.getLocalPart()) {
                    case "uname": return user;  // user-supplied name
                    case "pwd"  : return pass;  // user-supplied password
                    default     : return null;
                }
            }
        });

        /* 2. Fixed, hard-coded XPath expression – no string concatenation     */
        String xpr =
            "/users/user[@name=$uname and @pass=$pwd]";

        /* 3. Evaluate                                                        */
        XPathExpression compiled = xpath.compile(xpr);
        return (org.w3c.dom.Node)
               compiled.evaluate(doc, XPathConstants.NODE);
    }
}
```

Why this is safe
----------------
• The XPath expression itself never changes; it is a constant string in the code.  
• The user-controlled data are passed to the engine as *values*, not as part of the query text, so they cannot alter the structure of the expression.  
• Therefore attempts such as  
   user = "' or '1'='1"  
   pass = "anything"  
  cannot escape the variable and will just be treated as literal text.

If your XPath engine does not support variables, you must at least escape user input before inserting it into the query (e.g., turn apostrophes into concat(...) fragments), but using variables—as shown above—is both simpler and safer.

Below is a compact, self-contained example that demonstrates the Strategy pattern in JavaScript.  
Our “fun-bank” lets an account swap **transfer-fee strategies** on the fly (e.g., “student promo”, “crypto-bro”, “classic corporate”).  
Focus on the core pattern, not a production-ready back-end.

```js
/******************************************************************
 * 1.  STRATEGY INTERFACE (convention only – JS lacks true interfaces)
 ******************************************************************/

/**
 * Every FeeStrategy must expose:
 *    getName()      => string   (for logs/UI)
 *    calc(amount)   => number   (fee for that transfer)
 */

/******************************************************************
 * 2.  CONCRETE STRATEGIES
 ******************************************************************/

// 2.1  – zero fee, used in marketing campaigns
class ZeroFeeStrategy {
  getName()              { return '🚀 Student Promo (0%)'; }
  calc(amount)           { return 0; }
}

// 2.2  – small fixed fee
class FlatFeeStrategy {
  constructor(flat = 1.0) { this.flat = flat; }
  getName()              { return `💰 Flat Fee ($${this.flat.toFixed(2)})`; }
  calc(amount)           { return this.flat; }
}

// 2.3  – percentage based (e.g. fintech / crypto exchange)
class PercentFeeStrategy {
  constructor(percent = 1.5) { this.percent = percent; }
  getName()              { return `📈 ${this.percent}% of transfer`; }
  calc(amount)           { return (amount * this.percent) / 100; }
}

/******************************************************************
 * 3.  CONTEXT – our BankAccount delegates fee logic to a strategy
 ******************************************************************/

class BankAccount {
  constructor(owner, balance = 0, feeStrategy = new FlatFeeStrategy()) {
    this.owner        = owner;
    this.balance      = balance;
    this.feeStrategy  = feeStrategy;
  }

  setFeeStrategy(strategy) {
    console.log(`\n🔀  ${this.owner} switched to: ${strategy.getName()}`);
    this.feeStrategy = strategy;
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    this.balance += amount;
    console.log(`+ $${amount.toFixed(2)} deposited ➜ balance: $${this.balance.toFixed(2)}`);
  }

  transfer(amount, toWho = 'Someone') {
    const fee   = this.feeStrategy.calc(amount);
    const total = amount + fee;

    if (total > this.balance) {
      console.log(`❌ Transfer of $${amount.toFixed(2)} failed (need $${total.toFixed(2)}, have $${this.balance.toFixed(2)})`);
      return;
    }

    this.balance -= total;
    console.log(
      `✅ $${amount.toFixed(2)} sent to ${toWho} | fee: $${fee.toFixed(2)} (${this.feeStrategy.getName()}) ➜ new balance: $${this.balance.toFixed(2)}`
    );
  }
}

/******************************************************************
 * 4.  DEMO – play in the console / Node.js
 ******************************************************************/

(function funBankDemo() {
  console.log('🪙  Welcome to FunBank!\n');

  const alice = new BankAccount('Alice', 100); // default FlatFee($1)
  alice.deposit(50);              // now 150

  // Transfer while on Flat Fee
  alice.transfer(20, 'Bob');      // pays $1 fee

  // Switch to promo strategy
  alice.setFeeStrategy(new ZeroFeeStrategy());
  alice.transfer(20, 'Claire');   // pays $0 fee

  // Switch again – high-risk crypto gateway (1.5%)
  alice.setFeeStrategy(new PercentFeeStrategy(1.5));
  alice.transfer(50, 'Dexter');   // fee = $0.75

  // Attempt an overdraft just for fun
  alice.transfer(500, 'Evil Corp'); // should fail
})();

/* SAMPLE OUTPUT
🪙  Welcome to FunBank!

+ $50.00 deposited ➜ balance: $150.00
✅ $20.00 sent to Bob | fee: $1.00 (💰 Flat Fee ($1.00)) ➜ new balance: $129.00

🔀  Alice switched to: 🚀 Student Promo (0%)
✅ $20.00 sent to Claire | fee: $0.00 (🚀 Student Promo (0%)) ➜ new balance: $109.00

🔀  Alice switched to: 📈 1.5% of transfer
✅ $50.00 sent to Dexter | fee: $0.75 (📈 1.5% of transfer) ➜ new balance: $58.25
❌ Transfer of $500.00 failed (need $507.50, have $58.25)
*/
```

What you just saw:

1. Strategies (ZeroFee, FlatFee, PercentFee) encapsulate **different algorithms** for calculating transfer costs.  
2. `BankAccount` is the **Context**; it holds a reference to a `FeeStrategy` and delegates all fee maths to it.  
3. Changing behaviour at runtime is a single call (`setFeeStrategy(newStrategy)`), zero `if/else` cascades inside `BankAccount`.

That’s the Strategy pattern—clean, flexible, and here, a little more fun than your average UML diagram!

