Below is a complete, self-contained Java program that:

1. Prompts the user to enter two positive integers.
2. Computes their greatest common divisor (GCD) using the Euclidean algorithm.
3. Displays the result.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GCDCalculator {

    // Euclidean algorithm for GCD
    private static int gcd(int a, int b) {
        // Ensure a >= b for simplicity
        if (a < b) {
            int temp = a;
            a = b;
            b = temp;
        }

        while (b != 0) {
            int remainder = a % b;
            a = b;
            b = remainder;
        }
        return a;  // 'a' contains the GCD when 'b' becomes 0
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        int num1 = 0;
        int num2 = 0;

        // Prompt until valid positive integers are entered
        while (true) {
            System.out.print("Enter the first positive integer: ");
            if (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
                num1 = scanner.nextInt();
                if (num1 > 0) break;
            } else {
                scanner.next(); // clear invalid input
            }
            System.out.println("Please enter a valid positive integer.");
        }

        while (true) {
            System.out.print("Enter the second positive integer: ");
            if (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
                num2 = scanner.nextInt();
                if (num2 > 0) break;
            } else {
                scanner.next(); // clear invalid input
            }
            System.out.println("Please enter a valid positive integer.");
        }

        int result = gcd(num1, num2);
        System.out.printf("The greatest common divisor of %d and %d is %d.%n",
                           num1, num2, result);
        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

To run:

1. Save the file as `GCDCalculator.java`.
2. Compile with `javac GCDCalculator.java`.
3. Execute using `java GCDCalculator`, then follow the prompts.

