Below is a self-contained example that turns an infinite sequence of prime numbers into an ordinary Rust iterator.

You can paste this into Cargo’s default src/main.rs, run cargo run, and you’ll see the first 20 primes printed.  
Because it implements the standard Iterator trait, you can use all the usual iterator adaptors (map, filter, take_while, fold, etc.) to build your own “prime stream” pipelines.

```rust
/// A lazy, infinite stream of prime numbers.
///
/// Internally it remembers every prime it has emitted so far.  
/// The next candidate `n` is tested only against primes ≤ √n, so
/// each call to `next()` is still fast enough for most uses.
struct Primes {
    // All primes found so far; always sorted.
    known: Vec<u64>,
    // Next integer candidate to test.
    cand: 2,
}

impl Primes {
    /// Start a fresh prime iterator.
    fn new() -> Self {
        Primes {
            known: Vec::new(),
            cand: 2,
        }
    }

    /// Test whether `n` is prime by dividing only by already known primes
    /// up to √n.
    fn is_prime(&self, n: u64) -> bool {
        let limit = (n as f64).sqrt() as u64;
        for &p in &self.known {
            if p > limit {
                break;
            }
            if n % p == 0 {
                return false;
            }
        }
        true
    }
}

// Turn `Primes` into a real iterator.
impl Iterator for Primes {
    type Item = u64;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        loop {
            let n = self.cand;
            self.cand += 1;

            if self.is_prime(n) {
                self.known.push(n);
                return Some(n);
            }
        }
    }
}

// Demonstration.
fn main() {
    // Print the first 20 primes.
    for p in Primes::new().take(20) {
        println!("{}", p);
    }

    // Show off iterator combinators: e.g. sum of all primes < 1_000
    let sum: u64 = Primes::new()
        .take_while(|&p| p < 1_000)
        .sum();
    println!("Sum of primes below 1000 = {}", sum);
}
```

Explanation of the core logic
1. The struct keeps two pieces of state:
   • `known`: every prime discovered so far.  
   • `cand`: the next integer to check.

2. `is_prime` needs only to divide by primes ≤ √n—thanks to the
   fundamental theorem of arithmetic—so the work per call stays modest.

3. `next()` advances `cand` until it finds the next prime, stores it in
   `known`, and yields it. Because `Primes` implements `Iterator`, the
   rest of Rust’s iterator ecosystem “just works,” giving you an idiomatic,
   lazily-evaluated prime number stream.

Below is a fully-self–contained, **plain-TypeScript** (“no-deps”) implementation of a very small, vanilla RNN that can be trained on a toy character–prediction task.

It is intentionally minimal and highly commented so you can see exactly what is happening at every step.  
Put everything in a file called `rnn.ts`, compile with `tsc rnn.ts`, then run with `node rnn.js`.

```ts
/**********************************************************************
 * Extremely small vanilla RNN in plain TypeScript
 *  – single hidden layer, tanh activation
 *  – trained with back-propagation-through-time + SGD
 *
 *  Task below: learn to predict the next character in the string
 *              “hello ”, looping forever ( “h e l l o  ” … )
 *********************************************************************/

//////////////////////
//  Small helpers   //
//////////////////////

type Vec = number[];
type Mat = number[][];

/* ---------- algebra utilities (non–optimized, O(n³) loops) -------- */

const zeros  = (n: number): Vec            => Array(n).fill(0);
const zerosM = (r: number, c: number): Mat => Array(r).fill(0).map(() => zeros(c));

const randomM = (r: number, c: number): Mat =>
  Array(r).fill(0).map(() =>
    Array(c).fill(0).map(() => (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * 0.08) /* small uniform */);

const dot = (A: Mat, B: Vec): Vec => {
  const out = zeros(A.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < A.length; ++i)
    for (let k = 0; k < B.length; ++k)
      out[i] += A[i][k] * B[k];
  return out;
};
const add  = (a: Vec, b: Vec) => a.map((v, i) => v + b[i]);
const sub  = (a: Vec, b: Vec) => a.map((v, i) => v - b[i]);
const mul  = (a: Vec, b: Vec) => a.map((v, i) => v * b[i]);      // Hadamard
const scaleV= (a: Vec, s: number) => a.map(v => v * s);

const outer = (a: Vec, b: Vec): Mat => {
  const m = zerosM(a.length, b.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; ++i)
    for (let j = 0; j < b.length; ++j)
      m[i][j] = a[i] * b[j];
  return m;
};
const addM = (A: Mat, B: Mat) => A.map((row, i) => row.map((v, j) => v + B[i][j]));
const scaleM = (A: Mat, s: number) => A.map(row => row.map(v => v * s));

/* ---------- activation & derivatives -------- */

const tanh    = (v: Vec): Vec => v.map(x => Math.tanh(x));
const dtanh   = (v: Vec): Vec => v.map(x => 1 - Math.tanh(x) ** 2);

const softmax = (v: Vec): Vec => {
  const max = Math.max(...v);                      // stabilise
  const exps = v.map(x => Math.exp(x - max));
  const sum  = exps.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  return exps.map(e => e / sum);
};

/* cross-entropy loss and its gradient w.r.t. logits */
const xent = (probs: Vec, targetIdx: number): number => -Math.log(probs[targetIdx]);

const dSoftmaxXent = (probs: Vec, targetIdx: number): Vec => {
  const grad = [...probs];
  grad[targetIdx] -= 1;
  return grad;           // derivative of cross-entropy ◦ softmax
};

/* gradient clip (simple l∞ clip) */
const clip = (v: Vec, bound = 5) =>
  v.map(x => Math.max(-bound, Math.min(bound, x)));

const clipM = (m: Mat, bound = 5) =>
  m.map(row => clip(row, bound));

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//                          The RNN Class                               //
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

class RNN {
  inputSize:  number;
  hiddenSize: number;
  outputSize: number;

  /* parameters */
  Wxh: Mat; Whh: Mat; Why: Mat;
  bh:  Vec; by:  Vec;

  /* constructor: weight initialisation */
  constructor(input: number, hidden: number, output: number) {
    this.inputSize  = input;
    this.hiddenSize = hidden;
    this.outputSize = output;

    this.Wxh = randomM(hidden, input);
    this.Whh = randomM(hidden, hidden);
    this.Why = randomM(output, hidden);

    this.bh  = zeros(hidden);
    this.by  = zeros(output);
  }

  /* Forward pass through a whole sequence.
     Inputs: array of one-hot column vectors (length = seqLen)
     Returns: {ps: array of softmax probs, hs: hidden states, loss} */
  forward(xs: Vec[], targets: number[]) {
    const hs: Vec[] = [];
    const ps: Vec[] = [];
    let hPrev = zeros(this.hiddenSize);
    let loss  = 0;

    xs.forEach((x, t) => {
      const hRaw = add( add( dot(this.Wxh, x),
                             dot(this.Whh, hPrev) ),
                        this.bh );
      const h = tanh(hRaw);
      const y = add( dot(this.Why, h), this.by);
      const p = softmax(y);

      loss += xent(p, targets[t]);

      hs.push(h);
      ps.push(p);
      hPrev = h;
    });
    return {ps, hs, loss};
  }

  /* Back-propagation through time (single sequence) */
  backward(xs: Vec[], targets: number[], hs: Vec[], ps: Vec[]) {
    /* initialise grads with zeros */
    let dWxh = zerosM(this.hiddenSize, this.inputSize);
    let dWhh = zerosM(this.hiddenSize, this.hiddenSize);
    let dWhy = zerosM(this.outputSize, this.hiddenSize);
    let dbh  = zeros( this.hiddenSize );
    let dby  = zeros( this.outputSize );

    let dhNext = zeros(this.hiddenSize);

    for (let t = xs.length - 1; t >= 0; --t) {
      /* -------- gradient on output -------- */
      const dy  = dSoftmaxXent(ps[t], targets[t]);          // (outputSize)
      dWhy = addM(dWhy, outer(dy, hs[t]));                  // (o,h)
      dby  = add(dby, dy);

      /* -------- backprop into hidden -------- */
      let dh = add( dot(transpose(this.Why), dy), dhNext ); // (hidden)
      const dhRaw = mul(dh, dtanh(hs[t]));                  // elementwise
      dbh  = add(dbh, dhRaw);
      dWxh = addM(dWxh, outer(dhRaw, xs[t]));               // (h,i)
      const hPrev = t > 0 ? hs[t-1] : zeros(this.hiddenSize);
      dWhh = addM(dWhh, outer(dhRaw, hPrev));

      dhNext = dot( transpose(this.Whh), dhRaw );
    }

    /* clip to help exploding grads */
    dWxh = clipM(dWxh);
    dWhh = clipM(dWhh);
    dWhy = clipM(dWhy);
    dbh  = clip( dbh );
    dby  = clip( dby );

    return {dWxh,dWhh,dWhy,dbh,dby};
  }

  /* parameter update: plain SGD */
  update(grads: any, lr = 1e-1) {
    const {dWxh,dWhh,dWhy,dbh,dby} = grads;

    this.Wxh = addM(this.Wxh, scaleM(dWxh, -lr));
    this.Whh = addM(this.Whh, scaleM(dWhh, -lr));
    this.Why = addM(this.Why, scaleM(dWhy, -lr));

    this.bh  = add(this.bh,  scaleV(dbh, -lr));
    this.by  = add(this.by,  scaleV(dby, -lr));
  }
}

/* helper to transpose 2-D array */
function transpose(A: Mat): Mat {
  const r = A.length, c = A[0].length;
  const out = zerosM(c, r);
  for (let i = 0; i < r; ++i)
    for (let j = 0; j < c; ++j)
      out[j][i] = A[i][j];
  return out;
}

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//                    Tiny character-prediction demo                    //
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

/* training data: repeat “hello ” forever */
const data = "hello ";
const chars = [...new Set(data)];           // distinct characters
const charToIx = new Map(chars.map((c,i)=>[c,i]));
const ixToChar = new Map(chars.map((c,i)=>[i,c]));
const vocab = chars.length;

/* one-hot encoder */
const oneHot = (idx: number): Vec => {
  const v = zeros(vocab);
  v[idx] = 1;
  return v;
};

const rnn = new RNN(vocab, 16, vocab);

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//                          Training loop                               //
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

let smoothLoss = -Math.log(1/vocab) * data.length; // initialise

for (let iter = 0, pos = 0; iter < 3000; ++iter, pos = (pos+1)%data.length) {

  /* -------- prepare input / target sequence (length = data.length)--- */
  const xs: Vec[] = [];
  const targets: number[] = [];
  for (let t=0; t<data.length; ++t){
    const ix = charToIx.get(data[(pos+t) % data.length])!;
    const tx = charToIx.get(data[(pos+t+1) % data.length])!;
    xs.push(oneHot(ix));
    targets.push(tx);
  }

  /* -------- forward / back / update ------------ */
  const {ps,hs,loss} = rnn.forward(xs, targets);
  const grads        = rnn.backward(xs, targets, hs, ps);
  rnn.update(grads, 1e-1);

  smoothLoss = smoothLoss * 0.99 + loss * 0.01;

  if (iter % 200 === 0) {
    console.log(`iter ${iter}, loss=${smoothLoss.toFixed(2)}; sample: `,
                sample(rnn, charToIx.get('h')!, 20).join(''));
  }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------
   Sampling: feed the RNN its own predictions
   ------------------------------------------------ */
function sample(net: RNN, seedIx: number, n: number): string[] {
  let h = zeros(net.hiddenSize);
  let x = oneHot(seedIx);
  const out: string[] = [ixToChar.get(seedIx)!];

  for (let t=0;t<n;++t){
    h = tanh( add( add( dot(net.Wxh,x),
                        dot(net.Whh,h) ),
                   net.bh ) );
    const y  = add( dot(net.Why,h), net.by );
    const p  = softmax(y);
    const ix = pick(p);
    x = oneHot(ix);
    out.push(ixToChar.get(ix)!);
  }
  return out;
}

/* Draw random index according to multinomial distribution p */
function pick(p: Vec): number {
  const r = Math.random();
  let acc = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < p.length; ++i) {
    acc += p[i];
    if (r < acc) return i;
  }
  return p.length - 1; // fallback
}
```

How to run
==========

1. Save to `rnn.ts`
2. `npm install -g typescript` (if you don’t have it)
3. `tsc rnn.ts` – this emits `rnn.js`
4. `node rnn.js`

Every ~200 iterations the script prints the training loss and a short sample generated from the network. After a couple of thousand iterations you should see the network reliably generate the repeating pattern `hello hello …`.

Things kept deliberately simple
===============================

• 100 % CPU-only JavaScript arrays, no BLAS/WebGL – ideal for learning, not for speed.  
• No batching, no fancy optimizers (just SGD).  
• Sequence length equals training string length for clarity; you can cut it shorter and roll hidden state between batches like in classic char-RNNs.  
• Gradient clipping is l∞, you may switch to l² / global norm.  
• The toy dataset is tiny – overfitting is expected. Replace with any sequence data you want!

