Below is a small, self-contained utility that does exactly what you asked for:

• read a JSON file  
• look for every occurrence of a given key, no matter how deeply it is nested (objects or arrays)  
• return an array whose elements contain  
  – the complete “location” (path) to that key  
  – the value stored at that key  

You can drop the two functions in any Node/Browser code-base.  
(If you already have the JSON as a JavaScript object, just skip the readJsonFile part and call findKeyOccurrences directly.)

```javascript
/* ------------------------------------------------------------
 * 1.  Load / parse a JSON file (Node.js).  Returns a JS object.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------ */
const fs = require('fs').promises;

async function readJsonFile(filePath) {
  const raw = await fs.readFile(filePath, 'utf8');
  return JSON.parse(raw);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------
 * 2.  Recurse through the structure looking for the key.
 *     Returns: [{ path: 'root.level1[2].foo', value: ... }, …]
 * ------------------------------------------------------------ */
function findKeyOccurrences(jsonObj, targetKey) {
  const results = [];

  function recurse(node, pathSoFar) {
    // Ignore everything that is not an object/array
    if (node === null || typeof node !== 'object') return;

    // Arrays: iterate with numeric indexes in the path.
    if (Array.isArray(node)) {
      node.forEach((item, idx) => recurse(item, [...pathSoFar, `[${idx}]`]));
      return;
    }

    // Objects: iterate over own enumerable keys
    for (const key of Object.keys(node)) {
      const newPath = [...pathSoFar, key];

      // If this is the key we’re looking for, record it
      if (key === targetKey) {
        results.push({
          path: newPath.join('.').replace('.[', '['), // cosmetic: "a.[0]" → "a[0]"
          value: node[key]
        });
      }

      // Drill down
      recurse(node[key], newPath);
    }
  }

  recurse(jsonObj, []);
  return results;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------
 * 3.  Convenience wrapper: give it a file path + key, get results
 * ------------------------------------------------------------ */
async function searchKeyInFile(filePath, key) {
  const jsonObj = await readJsonFile(filePath);
  return findKeyOccurrences(jsonObj, key);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------
 * 4.  Example usage
 * ------------------------------------------------------------ */
(async () => {
  // Suppose "sample.json" is:
  // {
  //   "id": 1,
  //   "payload": {
  //     "user": { "name": "Ada", "id": 42 },
  //     "items": [
  //       { "id": 10, "name": "A" },
  //       { "id": 11, "name": "B" }
  //     ]
  //   }
  // }

  const hits = await searchKeyInFile('sample.json', 'id');
  console.log(hits);
})();

/* Console output:
[
  { path: 'id',                 value: 1 },
  { path: 'payload.user.id',    value: 42 },
  { path: 'payload.items[0].id', value: 10 },
  { path: 'payload.items[1].id', value: 11 }
]
*/
```

Notes / customisation:

1. Browser use-case: remove the `fs` portion and feed your already-parsed object directly to `findKeyOccurrences`.
2. Path formatting: here a dot (`.`) separates object keys, and array indices are written as `[i]`. Tweak `path.join` / `replace` if you prefer something else (e.g. JSON-Pointer style `/payload/items/0/id`).
3. Complexity: O(N) time and O(D) stack/heap, where N is the total number of nodes and D is the maximum depth.

Swift lets you add Boolean conditions to a pattern match in four main ways:

1. A where-clause that is part of the pattern inside a switch, for, if-case, guard-case or while-case statement.  
2. Chaining an ordinary Boolean condition after a pattern with a comma (often used in if-case / guard-case).  
3. Using the optional-binding sugar (if let / guard let) – this is really a pattern match for the case .some.  
4. Matching inside the Swift “case-family” keywords themselves: switch, if case, guard case, while case, for case.

Below are the syntaxes and some idiomatic examples.

────────────────────────────────────────
1. switch + where

let number = 7

switch number {
case 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19:
    print("prime under 20")

case let x where x % 2 == 0:
    print("even but not one of the above")

default:
    print("odd composite")
}

The let x “binds” the matched value; the trailing where x % 2 == 0 adds an extra Boolean test.

Tuples work the same way:

switch (x, y) {
case let (u, v) where u == v:
    print("on the line y = x")

case (0, _):
    print("on the y-axis")

default:
    break
}

────────────────────────────────────────
2. if case / guard case + comma condition

enum Result { case success(Int), failure(Error) }
let r: Result = .success(120)

if case let .success(value) = r, value > 100  {   // pattern, then Boolean
    print("big success → \(value)")
}

guard case .success(let v) = r, v > 100 else {
    fatalError("not a big enough success")
}

While or for loops are identical:

while case let .success(v) = nextResult(), v != 0 {
    print("stream value:", v)
}

for case let .success(v) in results where v > 0 {
    print(v)
}

────────────────────────────────────────
3. Optional binding is just pattern matching

// classic sugar
if let n = Int(textField.text!), n.isMultiple(of: 2) {
    print("\(n) is even and came from text-field")
}

// de-sugared form — same thing written explicitly
if case let .some(n) = Int(textField.text!), n.isMultiple(of: 2) {
    …
}

You can combine several optionals in a single pattern:

if case let (.some(x), .some(y)) = (a, b), x > y {
    …
}

────────────────────────────────────────
4. Pattern-matching keywords recap

• switch expr { case PATTERN… }  
• if   case PATTERN = expr { … } [, BooleanCondition]  
• guard case PATTERN = expr else { … } [, BooleanCondition]  
• while case PATTERN = expr { … } [, BooleanCondition]  
• for   case PATTERN in sequence { … } [where BooleanCondition]

Any PATTERN can contain additional where clauses, bindings (let/var), wild-cards (_), literals, enum-cases, tuples, etc.

────────────────────────────────────────
Take-away cheat-sheet

switch value {
case let binding where ExtraBool:
    …          // “where” lives inside the case
}

if case let PATTERN = expr, ExtraBool {
    …          // pattern first, comma-separated Boolean afterwards
}

guard case PATTERN = expr, ExtraBool else { … }

for case PATTERN in sequence where ExtraBool { … }

Optional-binding (if let / guard let) is just a pre-made “case .some” pattern.

With these building blocks you can mix Swift’s expressive pattern matching with ordinary Boolean logic wherever you need conditional control-flow.

```javascript
/**
 * retryRequest invokes an asynchronous function (e.g. fetch, axios, etc.)
 * up to 3 times, returning a single promise that:
 *   • resolves with the first successful result
 *   • rejects after the 3rd consecutive failure
 *
 * No async/await is used; the implementation relies only on .then/.catch
 * recursion.
 *
 * @param {Function} fn  A function that returns a promise (e.g. () => fetch(url))
 * @param {Number}  triesLeft  How many attempts are still allowed (default 3)
 * @return {Promise<any>}  The promise representing the eventual result
 */
function retryRequest(fn, triesLeft = 3) {
  return fn().then(
    result => result,                       // success: resolve immediately
    error  => {                             // failure: decide to retry or reject
      if (triesLeft <= 1) {
        // Out of retries → propagate the rejection
        return Promise.reject(error);
      }
      // Otherwise recurse, chaining the next attempt to the same promise
      return retryRequest(fn, triesLeft - 1);
    }
  );
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Example usage                                                      */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

// Wrap fetch (or any other async call) in a zero-arg function
const getData = () => fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1');

// Call it with automatic retries
retryRequest(getData, 3)
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(console.log)        // data after a successful attempt
  .catch(console.error);    // only reached after 3 consecutive failures
```

How it works
1. `retryRequest` executes the supplied function `fn` and immediately returns the promise it creates.
2. If the promise resolves, the outer promise resolves with the same value.
3. If the promise rejects:
   • When no retries remain, the rejection is propagated.  
   • Otherwise, the function calls itself recursively with `triesLeft - 1`; the returned promise is chained so the caller still receives a single unified promise.

Use a “multi-site / multi-tenant” set-up.

The idea in a nutshell  
• All domains resolve to the same server and the same code.  
• The request’s Host header (e.g. siteA.com vs siteB.com) tells the application which “tenant” is being served.  
• The application then chooses the right theme / template while reading and writing to the very same database.

How to do it, step by step

1. DNS  
   Point every domain at the same public IP address (A record) or CNAME them to the same host.

2. Web-server virtual hosts (Apache / Nginx / IIS)  
   Create one vhost entry per domain, but have every vhost use the exact same document root.  
   – You can also set an environment variable that tells the application which theme to load.

   Apache example:
   ```
   <VirtualHost *:80>
       ServerName  siteA.com
       ServerAlias www.siteA.com
       DocumentRoot /var/www/myapp          # same code for every site
       SetEnv THEME siteA                   # <- tell the app which look & feel to use
   </VirtualHost>

   <VirtualHost *:80>
       ServerName  siteB.com
       ServerAlias www.siteB.com
       DocumentRoot /var/www/myapp
       SetEnv THEME siteB
   </VirtualHost>
   ```

   Nginx example:
   ```
   server {
       server_name siteA.com www.siteA.com;
       root /var/www/myapp;
       fastcgi_param THEME siteA;
       ...
   }
   server {
       server_name siteB.com www.siteB.com;
       root /var/www/myapp;
       fastcgi_param THEME siteB;
       ...
   }
   ```

   (If you don’t want to touch vhost configs for every new domain, you can skip the SetEnv/FastCGI param and instead let the app read `Host:` from the request.)

3. Application logic  
   In your code inspect the host header or the environment variable and load the right theme.

   PHP:
   ```php
   // Either via Apache SetEnv
   $theme = getenv('THEME') ?: 'default';

   // …or infer from host header
   $host  = $_SERVER['HTTP_HOST'];
   switch ($host) {
       case 'siteA.com':
       case 'www.siteA.com': $theme = 'siteA'; break;
       case 'siteB.com':
       case 'www.siteB.com': $theme = 'siteB'; break;
       default:              $theme = 'default';
   }

   include "themes/$theme/header.php";
   ```
   Node/Express:
   ```js
   app.use((req, res, next) => {
       const map = { 'sitea.com': 'siteA', 'siteb.com': 'siteB' };
       res.locals.theme = map[req.hostname] || 'default';
       next();
   });
   ```

4. Database  
   • All sites share the same database connection string.  
   • If you need per-site data separation store a `site_id` column in your tables, or put site-specific info (e.g., theme name, logo path) in a `sites` table keyed by the domain.  
   • Otherwise just use the same tables and rows for everybody.

5. Static assets (optional)  
   Put each theme’s CSS, images, JS, etc., in a folder like `/themes/siteA/...`, `/themes/siteB/...` and build the asset URL using the theme name you selected above.

Advantages  
• One code base to maintain.  
• Adding a new “site” can be as easy as adding a DNS record and a row in the `sites` table.  
• Bug fixes and new features roll out to every site instantly.

Major frameworks already have a helper for this  
• WordPress Multisite, Drupal “multisite,” Django’s `django.contrib.sites`, Rails’ Apartment gem, Laravel’s Tenancy, etc. leverage exactly the pattern described above.

That’s all there is to it: multiple domains come in, the web server hands every request to the same application, the app looks at the host to decide which theme to use, and the database remains one and the same.

