Below is the smallest possible “Hello World”-style iMessage App Extension that shows two buttons when the extension is displayed in the compact (keyboard-sized) area of Messages.  
Everything is written in Swift; no storyboards are used so you can paste the file straight into a brand-new target and run.

──────────────────
1. Create the target
──────────────────
1. In Xcode choose File ▸ New ▸ Target ▸ iMessage Extension.  
2. Call it (for example) “TwoButtonsExtension”.  
   Xcode will generate a `MessagesViewController.swift` file for you – delete its contents and replace it with the code shown in section 2.

──────────────────
2. MessagesViewController.swift
──────────────────
import UIKit
import Messages

final class MessagesViewController: MSMessagesAppViewController {

    // MARK: – UI elements
    private let leftButton: UIButton = {
        let b = UIButton(type: .system)
        b.setTitle("Left", for: .normal)
        b.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
        return b
    }()

    private let rightButton: UIButton = {
        let b = UIButton(type: .system)
        b.setTitle("Right", for: .normal)
        b.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
        return b
    }()

    // MARK: – View lifecycle
    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()

        view.backgroundColor = .systemBackground
        setupLayout()
        wireActions()
    }

    // MARK: – Layout
    private func setupLayout() {
        view.addSubview(leftButton)
        view.addSubview(rightButton)

        NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
            // Left button
            leftButton.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.leadingAnchor, constant: 12),
            leftButton.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.centerYAnchor),

            // Right button
            rightButton.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.trailingAnchor, constant: -12),
            rightButton.centerYAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.centerYAnchor)
        ])
    }

    // MARK: – Actions
    private func wireActions() {
        leftButton.addTarget(self, action: #selector(leftTapped),  for: .touchUpInside)
        rightButton.addTarget(self, action: #selector(rightTapped), for: .touchUpInside)
    }

    @objc private func leftTapped()  { send(text: "👈 Left button tapped") }
    @objc private func rightTapped() { send(text: "👉 Right button tapped") }

    // Helper that inserts a message bubble into the conversation
    private func send(text: String) {
        guard let conversation = activeConversation else { return }
        let message = MSMessage()
        let layout  = MSMessageTemplateLayout()
        layout.caption = text
        message.layout = layout
        conversation.insert(message) { _ in }
    }
}

──────────────────
3. Build & run
──────────────────
• Choose the extension scheme (it ends in “MessagesExtension”)  
• Select “iOS Simulator › Messages”.  
• Hit ⌘-R.  
When the extension opens inside Messages you’ll see two buttons in the compact (keyboard-height) view; tapping either one simply drops a bubble into the conversation so you can verify that the tap worked.

──────────────────
What you just got
──────────────────
• A fully working iMessage extension.  
• No storyboards or nibs; everything is done in code for clarity.  
• Two buttons, each centered vertically and anchored to the leading/trailing edge.  
• A minimal action that shows how to interact with the current conversation (insert a message).

Below is the simplest practical way to make a browser start a “real” download ­— i.e. generate the familiar “save as …” dialog or place the file in the Downloads folder ­— using nothing but code you paste in the DevTools → Console.

How it works  
1. We pull the resource with fetch.  
2. We turn the response into a Blob (binary bucket).  
3. We create a temporary `<a>` element that points at that Blob and give it a download filename.  
4. We programmatically click the link, making the browser download the file.  
5. We clean up the temporary objects.

Code  

```js
// Paste everything in the console first, THEN call download()
// -----------------------------------------------------------

async function download(url, filename = undefined) {
  try {
    // 1. Get the remote file (needs CORS permission!)
    const response = await fetch(url);

    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);

    // 2. Response → Blob
    const blob = await response.blob();

    // 3. Build a temporary object-URL and <a> tag
    const objectUrl = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    const a = document.createElement('a');
    a.href      = objectUrl;
    // use provided name or fall back to last path segment or 'download'
    a.download  = filename || url.split('/').pop() || 'download';

    // 4. Trigger the download
    a.click();

    // 5. House-keeping
    URL.revokeObjectURL(objectUrl);
    console.log(`Download started → ${a.download}`);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Download failed:', err);
  }
}

/* ---- usage examples ------------------------------------ */
// Plain file
download('https://example.com/report.pdf');

// Give an explicit name
download('https://example.com/assets/logo.svg', 'my-logo.svg');

// Save an HTML page as raw source
download('https://example.org/page.html', 'page.html');
```

Important notes / gotchas
• Same-origin / CORS: The server you’re downloading from must either be the same origin as the page you’re on or send a permissive `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header (e.g. `*`). Otherwise `fetch` will be blocked.  
• Very large files: For huge files (hundreds of MB) the Blob sits in memory. For ad-hoc downloads that is usually fine; otherwise use a browser extension or a command-line tool.  
• Authentication / cookies: `fetch` uses the current tab’s cookies and credentials, so it works with sites you are signed in to, provided CORS still allows it.  
• Cannot spoof “Save link as …” without user gesture on some locked-down pages; the created click counts as a gesture in Chrome/Firefox, so the code above still works in most cases.

With that, you can “direct-download” almost any resource straight from the console.

Below is a minimal but fully-working Rust program that

• takes a text file that contains one URL per line,  
• separates YouTube links from every thing else,  
• downloads the YouTube links with yt-dlp (which must be installed and on the PATH),  
• prints the non-YouTube links so you can do something else with them later.

────────────────────  Cargo.toml ────────────────────
[package]
name = "url_splitter"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
url = "2"             # Robust URL parsing
anyhow = "1"          # Simple unified error type
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

────────────────────  src/main.rs ───────────────────
use std::{
    env,
    fs::File,
    io::{BufRead, BufReader},
    path::Path,
    process::{Command, Stdio},
};

use anyhow::{bail, Context, Result};
use url::Url;

fn main() -> Result<()> {
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1. Read file name from the command line
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    let input_path = env::args().nth(1).unwrap_or_else(|| {
        eprintln!("Usage: url_splitter <path-to-file-with-urls>");
        std::process::exit(1);
    });

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2. Load & iterate over the lines
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    let file = File::open(&input_path)
        .with_context(|| format!("Failed to open input file: {input_path}"))?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    let mut youtube_urls = Vec::<String>::new();
    let mut other_urls = Vec::<String>::new();

    for (idx, line_res) in reader.lines().enumerate() {
        let line = line_res.with_context(|| format!("Could not read line {idx}"))?;
        let trimmed = line.trim();

        if trimmed.is_empty() || trimmed.starts_with('#') {
            continue; // Skip empty/comment lines
        }

        // Use url crate for proper parsing; fall back to naive match on failure
        match Url::parse(trimmed) {
            Ok(parsed) => {
                if is_youtube(&parsed) {
                    youtube_urls.push(trimmed.to_owned());
                } else {
                    other_urls.push(trimmed.to_owned());
                }
            }
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("WARNING: Skipping malformed url `{trimmed}`: {e}");
            }
        }
    }

    println!("Found {} YouTube URLs and {} other URLs.\n", youtube_urls.len(), other_urls.len());

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3. Download each YouTube URL using yt-dlp
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    if youtube_urls.is_empty() {
        println!("No YouTube links to download – done.");
        return Ok(());
    }

    for url in &youtube_urls {
        println!("▶️  Downloading {url}");
        match spawn_ytdlp(url) {
            Ok(status) if status.success() => {
                println!("✅  Finished {url}\n");
            }
            Ok(status) => {
                eprintln!("❌  yt-dlp exited with status {status}");
            }
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("❌  Failed to run yt-dlp on {url}: {e}");
            }
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4. Do something with non-YouTube URLs (here we just print them)
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    if !other_urls.is_empty() {
        println!("Non-YouTube URLs:");
        for url in other_urls {
            println!("  {url}");
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper: check if a parsed Url points to YouTube
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
fn is_youtube(u: &Url) -> bool {
    matches!(
        u.domain(),
        Some("youtube.com" | "youtu.be" | "www.youtube.com" | "m.youtube.com")
    )
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper: spawn yt-dlp
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
fn spawn_ytdlp(url: &str) -> Result<std::process::ExitStatus> {
    // Feel free to append extra yt-dlp args, e.g. "--embed-metadata"
    let status = Command::new("yt-dlp")
        .arg(url)
        .stdin(Stdio::null())
        .status()
        .context("Failed to invoke yt-dlp")?;

    if !status.success() {
        bail!("yt-dlp failed");
    }
    Ok(status)
}
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

How to run:

1. Install yt-dlp in your shell (pip install -U yt-dlp or from your package manager).  
2. Put URLs (one per line) in urls.txt, e.g.

   https://youtu.be/dQw4w9WgXcQ
   https://example.com/article
   # a comment line
   https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oHg5SJYRHA0

3. Build & run:

   cargo run --release -- urls.txt

The program will download the two YouTube videos via yt-dlp and list the non-YouTube link separately.

Below is a self-contained console program that

1. loads an X.509 client certificate (stored in a PFX file),
2. attaches that certificate to an `HttpClient`,
3. sends an asynchronous POST request, and
4. prints the server’s response.

Compile with the current .NET SDK (`dotnet build`) and run (`dotnet run`).  
Replace the URI, certificate path, password, and request body with values that match your environment.

```csharp
// File: Program.cs
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace ClientCertificatePost
{
    class Program
    {
        // Entry point
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            // 1. Location of the PFX (PKCS#12) file containing your certificate and private key.
            //    You may keep the password in a secure store instead of embedding it in code.
            const string certPath     = @"c:\certs\myClientCert.pfx";
            const string certPassword = "pfx-password";

            // 2. Load the client certificate.
            X509Certificate2 clientCert;
            try
            {
                clientCert = new X509Certificate2(
                    certPath,
                    certPassword,
                    // MachineKeySet avoids “Key not valid for use in specified state” on some OSes
                    X509KeyStorageFlags.MachineKeySet |
                    X509KeyStorageFlags.EphemeralKeySet  // don’t persist key outside this process
                );
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Failed to load certificate: {ex.Message}");
                return;
            }

            // 3. Prepare an HTTP handler that knows about our certificate.
            var handler = new HttpClientHandler
            {
                ClientCertificateOptions = ClientCertificateOption.Manual
            };
            handler.ClientCertificates.Add(clientCert);

            // (Optional) Relax TLS validation for test environments ONLY.
            // Remove this in production!
            handler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback =
                (request, cert, chain, errors) => true;

            // 4. Create an HttpClient that uses the handler.
            using var http = new HttpClient(handler)
            {
                Timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30)
            };

            // 5. Craft the POST request.
            const string requestUri = "https://api.example.com/secure/endpoint";
            var jsonBody            = "{\"name\":\"Alice\",\"age\":34}";
            var content             = new StringContent(jsonBody, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

            try
            {
                // 6. Send the request and await the response.
                HttpResponseMessage response = await http.PostAsync(requestUri, content);

                Console.WriteLine($"Status : {(int)response.StatusCode} {response.ReasonPhrase}");
                Console.WriteLine("Headers: " + response.Headers);
                Console.WriteLine("----------------------------------");

                string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                Console.WriteLine(responseBody);
            }
            catch (HttpRequestException ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("HTTP error while calling the server:");
                Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
            }
            catch (TaskCanceledException)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("The request timed out.");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

How it works (quick recap):

• `X509Certificate2` loads the client certificate from a PFX file that contains both the certificate and its private key.  
• `HttpClientHandler.ClientCertificates.Add(cert)` tells the underlying TLS stack to present that certificate during the TLS handshake whenever the server requests it.  
• `HttpClient` performs the actual POST (`PostAsync`).  
• The program finally prints both status code and body.

That is all that is required to send a certificate-authenticated POST request in C#.

Below is a compact, self-contained way to pull every hashtag out of a piece of text with C#.  After the code you will find notes about how (and why) the regular expression is built, plus a few common variations you can drop in if your rules differ.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public static class HashTagHelper
{
    // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Extracts the hashtags (minus the ‘#’) from any piece of text.
    //      • Duplicates are removed    (use `.ToList()` instead of `.Distinct()`
    //        if you want them left in the order they appear)
    //      • Matching is case-insensitive
    //      • Works with Unicode letters as well as ASCII
    // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    public static IEnumerable<string> Extract(string text)
    {
        const string pattern =
            @"(?<=^|\s|[.,:;!?()""'])#          # the hash that starts a tag
              (?<tag>                           # capture only the tag part
                 \p{L}                          # start with any letter
                 [\p{L}\p{N}_]*                 # followed by letters, numbers, _
              )";

        return Regex.Matches(text, pattern, RegexOptions.IgnorePatternWhitespace)
                    .Cast<Match>()
                    .Select(m => m.Groups["tag"].Value)      // drop the ‘#’
                    .Distinct(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
    }
}

/* -------------------  Example usage  ------------------- */
public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var txt = "Hello #World!  This is a #CSharp demo.  Try #dotnet6  #CSharp.";
        foreach (var tag in HashTagHelper.Extract(txt))
            Console.WriteLine(tag);
    }
}
```

Output
```
World
CSharp
dotnet6
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
How the pattern works (high-level)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. `(?<=^|\s|[.,:;!?()""'])`  
   A *positive look-behind* that just says “in front of our `#` there must either be  
   • the start of the string (`^`),  
   • whitespace (`\s`) or  
   • one of the punctuation marks in the class `[.,:;!?()""']`.  
   That stops us matching the `#` inside an URL like `https://contoso.com/#anchor`.

2. `#`  
   The (literal) hash sign that starts the tag.

3. `(?<tag> … )`  
   A named capture group so we can pull the tag out without the leading `#`.

4. `\p{L}`  
   First character: any Unicode letter (ASCII `A-Z`/`a-z` plus accents, Cyrillic, etc.).

5. `[\p{L}\p{N}_]*`  
   Zero or more letters, numbers, or underscores.  
   The underscore is included because it is frequently allowed by social sites.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Common alterations
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
• If you need ASCII-only, swap `\p{L}\p{N}` with `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]`.  
• To forbid trailing underscores, replace the body with `\p{L}(?:[\p{L}\p{N]_]*\p{L}|\p{N})?`  
  (first char letter, last char letter/number).  
• To *keep* duplicates, remove `.Distinct(...)`.  
• To preserve the `#` outside, use `m.Value` instead of `m.Groups["tag"].Value`.  
• To strip trailing punctuation like “Hello #tag,” you can allow punctuation in the look-ahead:  
  `@"#(?<tag>\p{L}[\p{L}\p{N}_]*)(?=[\s.,;!?]|$)"`.

Feel free to adjust the pattern for your exact definition of “hashtag,” but the snippet above is usually adequate for Tweets, Mastodon posts, Instagram captions, etc.

Below is the quickest way to “wire-up” that button so it will

1. Grab the text that the operator types in the “User Name” box.  
2. POST it to `/api/users/{userName}/devices/d2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002`.  
3. (Optionally) insert a new row in the table when the request succeeds.

Add an `id` to the button, drop a small script block anywhere after the button (end of page, or inside a separate .js file), and you’re done.

```html
<div class="input-group mb-3">
    <!-- 1️⃣ give the button an id -->
    <button id="addUserBtn" class="btn btn-primary" type="button">
        <i class="bi bi-person-add"></i> Add User
    </button>  
    <input type="text" class="form-control" id="user_name" placeholder="User Name">
</div>

<!-- -------------------- JavaScript -------------------- -->
<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {

    const ADD_USER_BTN = document.getElementById('addUserBtn');
    const USER_INPUT   = document.getElementById('user_name');
    const TABLE_BODY   = document.querySelector('#userTable tbody');
    const FIXED_KEY    = 'd2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002';

    ADD_USER_BTN.addEventListener('click', async () => {

        const userName = USER_INPUT.value.trim();
        if (!userName) {           // simple validation
            alert('Please enter a user name');
            USER_INPUT.focus();
            return;
        }

        const url = `/api/users/${encodeURIComponent(userName)}/devices/${FIXED_KEY}`;

        try {
            const res = await fetch(url, { method : 'POST' });

            if (!res.ok) {                    // the server returned 4xx / 5xx
                const msg = await res.text(); // or res.json() depending on API
                throw new Error(msg || res.statusText);
            }

            // If your API returns JSON you can read it here
            // const data = await res.json();

            // 🟢 Success – update UI
            addRow(userName, FIXED_KEY);
            USER_INPUT.value = '';            // reset field
        }
        catch (err) {
            console.error(err);
            alert('Unable to add user: ' + err.message);
        }
    });

    // helper that adds a row to the table
    function addRow(name, key) {
        const tr = document.createElement('tr');
        tr.innerHTML = `
            <td>${name}</td>
            <td>${key}</td>
            <td>-</td>
            <td>added</td>`;
        TABLE_BODY.appendChild(tr);
    }

});
</script>
```

Explanation of the important parts:

• `encodeURIComponent(userName)` – protects you from spaces or special characters in the user-name.  
• `fetch(url, { method : 'POST' })` – performs the same HTTP POST your `curl` command did.  
• `res.ok` – is `true` for status 200–299; anything else triggers the error branch.  
• `addRow` – very small helper that injects a new `<tr>` into your table so the user can see the result immediately (optional; remove it if you don’t need it).  

If the API lives on a different host/port than the page that serves this HTML, replace the leading `/` with the full address (`http://localhost:5000/api/...`), or configure a proxy so both run on the same origin to avoid CORS issues.

