In nixpkgs every major LLVM release has its own attribute set, e.g.  
llvmPackages_15, llvmPackages_16, llvmPackages_17, …  
(the “plain” llvmPackages points to the default version of the
channel).

So the trick is simply to ask for the attribute set that corresponds
to version 17.

One–shot interactive shell
--------------------------

If you just want an ad-hoc shell that contains **all** of the LLVM-17
tools (clang, lld, lldb, opt, …):

    $ nix shell nixpkgs#llvmPackages_17          # with flakes
    # or
    $ nix-shell -p llvmPackages_17               # without flakes

Need only some of them?

    $ nix shell nixpkgs#llvmPackages_17.clang nixpkgs#llvmPackages_17.lld
    # or
    $ nix-shell -p 'llvmPackages_17.clang' -p 'llvmPackages_17.lld'

Permanent dev-shell (shell.nix)
-------------------------------

```nix
# shell.nix
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:

pkgs.mkShell {
  buildInputs = with pkgs.llvmPackages_17; [
    llvm   # the libraries and llvm-* bin utilities
    clang  # the C/C++/ObjC compiler
    lld    # linker
    lldb   # debugger
  ];
}
```

Flake-based dev-shell
---------------------

```nix
{
  description = "Dev shell with LLVM 17";

  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs";   # choose the branch you like

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }:
  let
    pkgs = nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${system};
  in {
    devShells.${system}.default = pkgs.mkShell {
      buildInputs = with pkgs.llvmPackages_17; [ llvm clang lld lldb ];
    };
  };
}
```

That’s all—no overlay or pinning needed as long as your nixpkgs
revision is recent enough to contain llvmPackages_17.

Below is a step-by-step recipe – with a complete but compact C# example – that shows the typical way people build a Windows service that passively sniffs and decodes packets with Npcap.

(The same ideas apply to C/C++, Delphi, Rust … only the service “plumbing” changes.)

─────────────────────
1. Install the prerequisites
─────────────────────
• Visual Studio 2019/2022 (Community is fine).  
• Npcap run-time: `npcap-<ver>.exe /S /loopback_support=yes /winpcap_mode=no`  
  – installs the kernel driver `NPF` and the user-mode DLL `wpcap.dll`.
• Npcap SDK (contains headers, `Packet.lib`, `wpcap.dll`).  
• NuGet packages (C# only):  

  SharpPcap   – thin .NET wrapper over WinPcap/Npcap  
  PacketDotNet – protocol decoder built on top of SharpPcap

─────────────────────
2. Create the service project
─────────────────────
File → New → Project → “Windows Service (.NET Framework)”.  
(Target .NET 4.7+; .NET 6/7 “Worker Service” also works, but Framework is a little simpler to show.)

Add NuGet references:

```
Install-Package SharpPcap
Install-Package PacketDotNet
```

─────────────────────
3. Wire up the service skeleton
─────────────────────
Service code lives in a class derived from `ServiceBase`.  
You generally:

• Spawn a background thread / Task in `OnStart`.  
• Open an Npcap device, go promiscuous, register packet callback.  
• Stop capture & clean up in `OnStop`.

Full code (Program.cs + SnifferService.cs) is shown next.

─────────────────────
Program.cs – regular Service entry point
─────────────────────
```csharp
using System.ServiceProcess;

namespace NpcapSnifferSvc
{
    static class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
#if DEBUG
            // Makes debugging easier: start as a console when run from VS
            var svc = new SnifferService();
            svc.DebugRun();                // <-- custom helper (see below)
#else
            ServiceBase.Run(new SnifferService());
#endif
        }
    }
}
```

─────────────────────
SnifferService.cs – all the interesting stuff
─────────────────────
```csharp
using System;
using System.ServiceProcess;
using System.Threading;
using SharpPcap;
using PacketDotNet;

namespace NpcapSnifferSvc
{
    public class SnifferService : ServiceBase
    {
        private ICaptureDevice _dev;
        private Thread _worker;

        public SnifferService()
        {
            ServiceName = "NpcapSnifferSvc";
        }

        protected override void OnStart(string[] args)
        {
            _worker = new Thread(CaptureLoop) { IsBackground = true };
            _worker.Start();
        }

        protected override void OnStop()
        {
            _dev?.StopCapture();
            _dev?.Close();
            _worker?.Join(2_000);
        }

        // Convenience for F5 debugging
        internal void DebugRun()
        {
            OnStart(null);
            Console.WriteLine("Press <enter> to stop ...");
            Console.ReadLine();
            OnStop();
        }

        // Packet capture + decode
        private void CaptureLoop()
        {
            try
            {
                //-------------------------------------------------
                // 1) Choose an interface
                //-------------------------------------------------
                var devices = CaptureDeviceList.Instance;
                if (devices.Count == 0)
                {
                    EventLog.WriteEntry("No Npcap interfaces found.");
                    return;
                }

                // Pick first non-loopback – adjust policy as you like
                _dev = Array.Find(devices.ToArray(),
                                  d => !d.Interface.FriendlyName.Contains("Loopback"));

                //-------------------------------------------------
                // 2) Open device in promiscuous mode
                //-------------------------------------------------
                const int readTimeoutMs = 1_000;
                _dev.Open(DeviceMode.Promiscuous, readTimeoutMs);

                //-------------------------------------------------
                // 3) Optionally set a capture filter
                //-------------------------------------------------
                _dev.Filter = "tcp port 80 or 443";        // BPF syntax

                //-------------------------------------------------
                // 4) Hook packet-arrival event
                //-------------------------------------------------
                _dev.OnPacketArrival += OnPacketArrival;

                //-------------------------------------------------
                // 5) Start pump (blocks until StopCapture)
                //-------------------------------------------------
                _dev.Capture();
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                EventLog.WriteEntry("CaptureLoop crashed:\r\n" + ex, EventLogEntryType.Error);
            }
        }

        // Actual decoding happens here
        private void OnPacketArrival(object sender, CaptureEventArgs e)
        {
            try
            {
                var raw = e.Packet;

                // Turn bytes into a PacketDotNet object graph
                var packet = Packet.ParsePacket(raw.LinkLayerType, raw.Data);

                // Example: extract IPv4/TCP
                var ip  = packet.Extract<IPv4Packet>();
                var tcp = packet.Extract<TcpPacket>();

                if (ip == null || tcp == null) return;

                string line =
                    $"{raw.Timeval.Date:HH:mm:ss.fff}  {ip.SourceAddress}:{tcp.SourcePort} → {ip.DestinationAddress}:{tcp.DestinationPort}  Len={tcp.PayloadData.Length}";
                EventLog.WriteEntry(line, EventLogEntryType.Information, 0, false);

                // If you care about the payload:
                // string payload = System.Text.Encoding.ASCII.GetString(tcp.PayloadData);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                EventLog.WriteEntry("Decode error:\r\n" + ex);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Notes:
• `Capture()` is a blocking loop. If you prefer non-blocking, call `StartCapture()` instead.  
• The service writes decoded lines to the Windows Event Log for demo purposes; in production you would forward them to a database, SIEM, queue, etc.

─────────────────────
4. Add an installer class (or use sc.exe)
─────────────────────
Option A – code-based:

```csharp
[RunInstaller(true)]
public class SnifferProjectInstaller : Installer
{
    public SnifferProjectInstaller()
    {
        var process = new ServiceProcessInstaller
        {
            Account = ServiceAccount.LocalSystem,  // needs SeLoadDriverPrivilege
        };
        var service = new ServiceInstaller
        {
            StartType   = ServiceStartMode.Automatic,
            ServiceName = "NpcapSnifferSvc",
            DisplayName = "Npcap Packet Sniffer",
            Description = "Decodes traffic using Npcap."
        };
        Installers.Add(process);
        Installers.Add(service);
    }
}
```

Run `InstallUtil.exe NpcapSnifferSvc.exe` (or `sc create …`) to register it.  
Start/stop with `sc start NpcapSnifferSvc`, `sc stop …`.

Option B – no installer class:

```
sc create NpcapSnifferSvc binPath= "C:\Path\NpcapSnifferSvc.exe" start= auto
```

─────────────────────
5. Permissions & “NPF” driver
─────────────────────
• The account that runs the service needs to open the `NPF` device.  
  – `LocalSystem` already has the privilege.  
  – If you run as NETWORK SERVICE or another user, add it to the “Npcap” service ACL:  
    `sc sdset npcap …` or use `Admin Tools → Services → Npcap → Properties → Log On`.  
• If your service is 32-bit on 64-bit Windows, ship both 32- and 64-bit copies of `wpcap.dll`.  
  (SharpPcap loads the one that matches CLR bitness.)

─────────────────────
6. Typical enhancements
─────────────────────
• Interface selection UI (registry key, config file, WMI property).  
• Handle interface hot-plug (re-enumerate on `DeviceArrival` WM).  
• Write raw PCAP rolls to disk with `CaptureFileWriterDevice`, send only metadata to SIEM.  
• Buffer / batch logs instead of writing per packet.  
• Use TLS fingerprinting, HTTP header parsing, DPI libraries, etc.  
• Sign your EXE and ship Npcap redistributable in silent mode (`/S`).  
• Unit-test packet decoders by feeding `.pcap` files into `OfflineDevice`.

─────────────────────
7. Troubleshooting checklist
─────────────────────
• Service starts, then stops instantly → probably threw in `OnStart`; look at Application Event Log.  
• “The handle is invalid” → account can’t open `\\Device\\NPF_*`.  
• Nothing captured → Right interface? Promiscuous allowed by switch? Check firewall/NIC offload.  
• High CPU → Don’t decode in callback; push to a queue and process in worker threads.

─────────────────────
8. The C/C++ alternative (ultra-short sketch)
─────────────────────
• `CreateService()` / `StartServiceCtrlDispatcher()` → `ServiceMain()` → spawn thread.  
• In thread: `pcap_findalldevs()`, `pcap_open_live()`, `pcap_setfilter()`, then  
  either `pcap_loop()` with callback or `pcap_next_ex()` in your own loop.  
• Link with `wpcap.lib` and `Packet.lib` from Npcap SDK.  
(Everything else – install, permissions, filter syntax – is the same.)

─────────────────────
That’s the whole pipeline: create the service, open Npcap, register the arrival handler, decode with PacketDotNet (or your own parser), package and deploy.

SVG already gives you everything you need; you just have to ask for it.

1. Grab the element whose box you want (it can be buried under any number of <g> tags).  
2. Call getBBox() to get its own rectangle.  
3. Call getScreenCTM() to see how that rectangle is transformed all the way up the SVG tree.  
4. Apply the matrix to the four corners and read off the final min/max X- and Y-values.  

Code (drop-in ready):

```js
function absoluteBBox(el){
  // 1) local box
  const b = el.getBBox();            // {x, y, width, height}

  // 2) matrix that maps local coords → screen coords
  const m = el.getScreenCTM();       // DOMMatrix

  // 3) corners transformed to screen space
  const pts = [
    new DOMPoint(b.x, b.y),
    new DOMPoint(b.x + b.width, b.y),
    new DOMPoint(b.x, b.y + b.height),
    new DOMPoint(b.x + b.width, b.y + b.height)
  ].map(p => p.matrixTransform(m));

  // 4) build bounding rectangle
  const xs = pts.map(p => p.x), ys = pts.map(p => p.y);
  return {
    left:   Math.min(...xs),
    top:    Math.min(...ys),
    width:  Math.max(...xs) - Math.min(...xs),
    height: Math.max(...ys) - Math.min(...ys)
  };
}

// example
const deep = document.querySelector('#mySvg g g path');
console.log( absoluteBBox(deep) );
```

absoluteBBox() returns the element’s bounding box in screen pixels, no matter how deeply it’s nested or transformed.

In Compose you do not “query the parent from the child” like you do with a CSS container-query.  
Instead you let the *parent* measure itself first and, still during that measurement
phase, decide which variant of the UI it is going to emit or how it is going to place its
children.

There are three practical ways to do it:

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1.  BoxWithConstraints – simplest for *content switching*
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BoxWithConstraints gives you the final width/height that the container is going to get.
Because you are still inside the composition phase you can simply branch and compose a
totally different subtree.

```kotlin
private val BreakpointCompact  = 0.dp          // anything ≥ 0
private val BreakpointMedium   = 600.dp        // tablet-portrait
private val BreakpointExpanded = 840.dp        // desktop/large tablet

@Composable
fun AdaptiveCard(
    article: Article,
    modifier: Modifier = Modifier
) {
    BoxWithConstraints(modifier) {
        val sizeClass = remember(maxWidth) {
            when {
                maxWidth < BreakpointMedium   -> SizeClass.Compact
                maxWidth < BreakpointExpanded -> SizeClass.Medium
                else                          -> SizeClass.Expanded
            }
        }

        when (sizeClass) {
            SizeClass.Compact  -> CompactCard(article)   // stacked Column
            SizeClass.Medium   -> MediumCard(article)    // Row with equal weights
            SizeClass.Expanded -> ExpandedCard(article)  // fancy two-pane layout
        }
    }
}
```

• The decision happens only when `maxWidth` changes, so recomposition is cheap.  
• You can completely reorder, add or remove children – very similar to CSS container
  queries.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2.  SubcomposeLayout – when you need to *measure first*  
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Sometimes you want to *try* one layout and fall back to another only if it actually
doesn’t fit (e.g. “put chips in one line, otherwise wrap them”).  
`SubcomposeLayout` lets you do exactly that: you can measure variant A, look at the
numbers, then sub-compose variant B if necessary.

```kotlin
@Composable
fun SmartRowOrColumn(
    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,
    horizontal: @Composable () -> Unit,
    vertical  : @Composable () -> Unit
) {
    SubcomposeLayout(modifier) { constraints ->

        // 1st attempt : horizontal
        val hPlaceables = subcompose("H", horizontal)
            .map { it.measure(constraints) }

        val hWidth  = hPlaceables.sumOf { it.width }
        val hHeight = hPlaceables.maxOfOrNull { it.height } ?: 0

        if (hWidth <= constraints.maxWidth) {
            // it fits -> actually lay it out
            layout(hWidth, hHeight) {
                var x = 0
                hPlaceables.forEach { p ->
                    p.placeRelative(x, 0)
                    x += p.width
                }
            }
        } else {
            // 2nd attempt : vertical
            val vPlaceables = subcompose("V", vertical)
                .map { it.measure(constraints) }

            val width  = vPlaceables.maxOfOrNull { it.width } ?: 0
            val height = vPlaceables.sumOf { it.height }

            layout(width, height) {
                var y = 0
                vPlaceables.forEach { p ->
                    p.placeRelative(0, y)
                    y += p.height
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Usage:

```kotlin
SmartRowOrColumn(
    horizontal = {
        Image(…)
        Spacer(Modifier.width(12.dp))
        Text("Title")
    },
    vertical = {
        Text("Title")
        Spacer(Modifier.height(8.dp))
        Image(…)
    }
)
```

The code is longer than CSS, but the idea is the same: *measure first, decide later*.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3.  Custom Layout – for pure *re-ordering / positioning*
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
If you do not need two completely different sub-trees, you can write a `Layout { … }`
and move children around depending on `constraints.maxWidth/maxHeight`.  
That is exactly how the standard `Row`, `Column`, `FlowRow`, etc. are implemented.

```kotlin
@Composable
fun ResponsiveRow(
    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,
    content: @Composable () -> Unit
) {
    Layout(
        content = content,
        modifier = modifier
    ) { measurables, constraints ->
        // Very naïve: if not all children fit next to each other, switch to vertical.
        val placeables = measurables.map { it.measure(constraints) }
        val totalWidth = placeables.sumOf { it.width }

        if (totalWidth <= constraints.maxWidth) {
            val height = placeables.maxOf { it.height }
            layout(totalWidth, height) {
                var x = 0
                placeables.forEach { p ->
                    p.placeRelative(x, 0)
                    x += p.width
                }
            }
        } else {
            val width  = placeables.maxOf { it.width }
            val height = placeables.sumOf { it.height }
            layout(width, height) {
                var y = 0
                placeables.forEach { p ->
                    p.placeRelative(0, y)
                    y += p.height
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Tips & gotchas
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
• You cannot ask the *child* how much room it will finally get; only the parent knows
  that. That is why the parent has to be the place where the switch occurs.  
• Use `remember` or `derivedStateOf` around your break-point logic so you do not
  recompute the branch on every recomposition.  
• If the decision only depends on *window* size, you can also use
  `WindowSizeClass` from material-3 adaptive libraries, but `BoxWithConstraints`
  keeps it local, like real container queries.  
• For layout changes you want to animate between, wrap the branching code inside
  `Crossfade` or use `LookaheadLayout`/`AnimatedContent`.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Putting it all together – generic helper
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
```kotlin
enum class SizeClass { Compact, Medium, Expanded }

@Composable
fun <T> ContainerQuery(
    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,
    breakpoints: List<Pair<Dp, SizeClass>> = listOf(
        600.dp to SizeClass.Medium,
        840.dp to SizeClass.Expanded
    ),
    content: @Composable (SizeClass) -> T
) {
    BoxWithConstraints(modifier) {
        val sizeClass = remember(maxWidth) {
            breakpoints
                .firstOrNull { maxWidth < it.first }
                ?.second ?: SizeClass.Expanded
        }
        content(sizeClass)
    }
}
```

Use it like this:

```kotlin
ContainerQuery { size ->
    when (size) {
        SizeClass.Compact  -> PhoneUI()
        SizeClass.Medium   -> TabletUI()
        SizeClass.Expanded -> DesktopUI()
    }
}
```

With these patterns you can replicate virtually every CSS container-query behaviour
inside Jetpack Compose.
